window.OC_SEARCH_INDEX=[{"u":"chapter-1.html#infographic-summary","t":"I N F O G R A P H I C S U M M A R Y","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Visual summary of this chapter — click to enlarge or download. Download infographic (PDF)"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#intro","t":"Introduction","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"This chapter introduces digital and media literacy as a foundational competence for young people navigating today’s complex digital environments. It explores how information is produced, circulated and consumed , and how young people can develop the critical orientation, ethical awareness and safety skills required to participate responsibly in digital spaces. Designed as a practical guide for youth workers, the chapter connects conceptual understanding with concrete learning tools , participatory activities and real-life examples from youth work practice. By combining theory and practice , it supports educators in helping young people move from passive media consumption toward critical awareness and responsible digital participation . It also promotes responsible and frugal digital practices , encouraging young people to produce and share content consciously , avoid unnecessary digital accumulation and engage online with greater purpose and care."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#understanding-digital-literacy-and-media-literacy-sintro","t":"Understanding digital literacy and media literacy","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Although the terms digital literacy and media literacy are often used together, they refer to slightly different competences. Digital literacy generally refers to the ability to use digital technologies effectively and responsibly . It includes skills such as navigating online platforms, understanding how digital tools work, protecting personal data and interacting safely in digital environments. Media literacy , on the other hand, focuses more specifically on understanding and critically analysing media content . It involves recognising how information is produced, identifying bias or manipulation , evaluating sources and understanding how media messages influence opinions and perceptions. In practice, these competences are closely interconnected . Young people need both digital skills and critical media understanding in order to navigate contemporary information environments responsibly."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#digcomp-3-0-connection-sintro","t":"DigComp 3.0 connection","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"This chapter mainly supports three areas of DigComp 3.0: Information search, evaluation and management Communication and collaboration Safety, wellbeing and responsible use"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#learning-objectives-sintro","t":"Learning objectives","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"By the end of this chapter, youth workers will be able to: understand digital and media literacy as more than a technical skill, emphasising orientation, critical thinking and responsibility support young people in recognising misinformation, manipulation and algorithmic influence facilitate safe, ethical and reflective digital participation apply practical tools and activities that strengthen critical media engagement use case studies and examples to connect theory with youth work practice. Sections Section 1 | Digital environments, media consumption & misinformation Section 2 | Key competences Section 3 | Practical tools & activities Section 4 | Case studies & best practices"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#section-1-digital-environments-media-consumption-misinformat","t":"Section 1 · Digital environments, media consumption & misinformation","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":""},{"u":"chapter-1.html#conceptual-framework-s1","t":"Conceptual framework","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"We wake up with screens, learn through screens, communicate through screens and increasingly understand the world through digital interfaces. News, opinions, images and narratives circulate continuously, often without clear boundaries between fact and interpretation, information and entertainment, or public and private communication. In such an environment, the central challenge is no longer access to information but orientation within it . Young people are surrounded by a constant flow of content and are frequently required to decide what to trust, what to share and how to respond . In everyday digital life, this situation becomes visible in simple moments: a viral TikTok trend spreads across feeds within hours, a screenshot of a message circulates rapidly through WhatsApp groups, or a platform repeatedly recommends similar videos based on previous interactions. These experiences illustrate how digital environments shape attention, influence perception and guide behaviour . Digital literacy emerges precisely at this intersection of opportunity and risk . While digital spaces offer unprecedented possibilities for participation, expression and connection, they also expose users to misinformation , manipulation and invisible algorithmic structures. Digital literacy therefore becomes a fundamental competence for navigating contemporary social, cultural and civic life."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#what-is-digital-literacy-today-s1","t":"1.1. What is digital literacy today?","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Digital literacy today begins with a simple reality: the digital world is no longer a place we occasionally visit; it is a space in which we continuously live . Many young people begin their day by checking a screen and end it the same way. Over time, this constant presence becomes almost invisible, yet it profoundly shapes how young people learn, communicate, form opinions and build relationships. For this reason, digital literacy cannot be defined only as “using technology well”. Knowing how to open an application, post a video or navigate a platform represents only the surface level of digital competence . The deeper challenge lies in recognising how digital environments influence attention, emotions, judgement and social interaction . Digital literacy therefore involves orientation: the ability to find direction within a dense and rapidly changing information landscape. Online information rarely arrives calmly or sequentially. Instead, it appears as a continuous stream of headlines, images, short videos, comments and notifications, many of which are designed to trigger immediate emotional reactions . In such conditions, the main risk is not only the presence of false information but the disappearance of reflection . When reactions become automatic, critical thinking often arrives too late. Digital literacy helps restore this pause by encouraging simple habits of questioning before believing or sharing information. Key questions include: Who created this content and for what purpose? What evidence is presented and what might be missing? Is the tone informative, persuasive or provocative? Is the information current or taken out of context? These questions do not aim to generate suspicion, but to support responsible judgement and more thoughtful decision-making."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#perceived-digital-competence-s1","t":"1.2. Perceived digital competence","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Another important challenge highlighted through the project’s survey and focus group discussions with young people is the widespread perception that many young participants already “know how to use digital media”. Because young people grow up surrounded by smartphones, social media platforms and messaging applications, digital competence is often associated with familiarity and speed rather than with critical understanding . Many participants feel confident navigating platforms, posting content or interacting online, which can create the impression that additional digital literacy education is unnecessary. However, the findings from the project’s research activities suggest a different reality. While young people generally demonstrate strong operational skills , such as using apps or creating content, they often feel less confident when it comes to evaluating information, recognising misinformation , understanding algorithmic influence or identifying manipulated media . These insights emerged from the project’s survey and focus group discussions conducted with young participants and youth workers across partner countries. The results highlight an important gap between digital usage and critical digital literacy . The full survey findings and qualitative insights from the focus groups are available in the project research report: European needs analysis This perceived competence can sometimes lead to overconfidence . When young people assume they already master digital environments, they may be less inclined to question sources, verify information or reflect on algorithmic influence. For youth workers, this insight is particularly important. Digital literacy education should therefore not assume that young people lack digital experience. Instead, it should help participants move from digital familiarity toward critical digital awareness , encouraging reflection on how digital environments shape information, behaviour and social interaction."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#algorithmic-influence-s1","t":"1.3. Algorithmic influence","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Digital environments do not simply display information; they organise it. Algorithms act as invisible maps that guide users through content by predicting what will keep them engaged. While this system may create personalised and convenient digital experiences, it also shapes what becomes visible and what remains hidden. Repetition can feel like truth , and familiarity can be mistaken for credibility . Over time, algorithmic filtering can create environments where: similar viewpoints are repeatedly reinforced unfamiliar perspectives become less visible popularity is confused with reliability Understanding this dynamic is a central component of digital literacy."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#ai-generated-content-and-the-challenge-of-authenticity-s1","t":"1.4. AI-generated content and the challenge of authenticity","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Recent developments in artificial intelligence have introduced new complexities into digital media environments. AI tools can now generate highly realistic images, videos, voices and texts that closely resemble authentic media. As a result, visual realism can no longer be treated as reliable evidence that a piece of content reflects a real event. A convincing video or image circulating online may be synthetic or manipulated through AI technologies. For young people navigating digital platforms, this creates new layers of uncertainty. Distinguishing between authentic documentation and generated content requires increased attention to sources, context and verification . Discussing AI-generated media in youth work settings helps young people understand that credibility in digital environments must be examined critically rather than assumed based on visual appearance. For many young people, AI tools are already part of everyday digital practices. For example, some youth use AI applications to generate images for social media posts, create edited photos or avatars, produce short videos or modify voices for entertainment content. Others may use AI tools to write captions, messages or school-related texts. While these uses can be creative and harmless, they also illustrate how easily digital content can be generated or altered. Discussing these everyday uses helps young people better understand how authenticity, authorship and credibility can become blurred in digital environments."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#implications-for-youth-work-practice-s1","t":"1.5. Implications for youth work practice","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"For youth workers, addressing misinformation should go beyond warning young people about risks or presenting long lists of rules. Instead, effective practice focuses on developing practical verification habits, collective reflection and critical awareness through structured learning activities. Within youth work settings, digital literacy activities can aim to achieve concrete and observable learning outcomes . For example, workshops and learning sessions may target the following objectives: enable participants to analyse at least 3 different types of digital content (news posts, videos, screenshots or memes) and discuss their credibility encourage young people to apply basic verification steps (source checking, reverse search, cross-referencing) before sharing information facilitate group discussions where participants compare different interpretations of the same digital content support young people in recognising algorithmic influence by analysing how recommendations or trending content appear in their own digital feeds strengthen awareness of AI-generated or manipulated media through guided examples and verification exercises Youth workers can integrate these practices into workshops, digital storytelling activities or media analysis sessions. A typical youth workshop may include: analysing 2–3 viral social media posts and identifying possible misinformation signals discussing how algorithmic recommendations shape attention and opinions reflecting collectively on why certain types of content spread faster than others Such exercises help transform digital literacy from abstract knowledge into practical analytical skills . In addition, youth work methodologies can promote collaborative learning by encouraging participants to: question information sources together compare perspectives in small groups reflect on emotional reactions to digital content practise pausing before sharing information online Through these collective processes, critical thinking becomes a shared competence developed through dialogue and experience, rather than an individual responsibility carried by each user alone. When youth workers create spaces where questioning, reflection and discussion are encouraged, young people gradually develop the confidence and skills required to navigate complex digital environments more responsibly."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#transition-to-the-following-chapters-s1","t":"Transition to the following chapters","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"These competences become especially meaningful when young people apply them in real cultural and social contexts. Within the OURCULTURE framework, digital literacy is understood as a foundational competence that enables young people to explore their cultural environments, document local heritage and share their experiences through digital media. Once young people develop critical awareness of digital media, they are better equipped to explore their cultural surroundings, document local heritage and transform everyday experiences into meaningful narratives . The following chapters therefore move from understanding digital environments toward documenting culture, developing storytelling practices and supporting youth-led digital media production. Key message Misinformation thrives where speed replaces reflection and emotion replaces context . Digital and media literacy responds not by controlling information, but by strengthening young people’s ability to orient themselves , question what they encounter, and participate consciously in complex media environments.`"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#section-2-key-competences","t":"Section 2 · Key competences","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":""},{"u":"chapter-1.html#information-evaluation-and-fact-checking-s2","t":"2.1. Information evaluation and fact-checking","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"In contemporary digital environments, information evaluation is no longer an occasional skill used only in formal learning contexts. It has become a daily competence required to navigate an uninterrupted stream of news, opinions, images and messages. Young people encounter information continuously, often without actively searching for it, which makes the ability to pause, assess and decide especially important. Information evaluation begins with orientation-the capacity to understand what kind of content one is encountering and why it appears at that moment. Before determining whether something is true or false, young people need to recognise whether the content aims to inform, persuade, entertain or provoke a reaction. This first step helps shift media use from automatic consumption to conscious engagement . Importantly, these skills are not only relevant when young people consume information. They are equally essential when young people create their own digital content-such as videos, podcasts, blog posts or social media stories. Understanding how information is framed, verified and shared enables young creators to produce responsible, trustworthy and meaningful media . In this sense, information evaluation becomes both a critical and a creative competence that supports youth-led storytelling and digital media production."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#evaluating-sources-and-intent-s2","t":"Evaluating sources and intent","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"A core element of information evaluation is the ability to question sources . This does not require advanced technical expertise, but rather consistent habits of inquiry . Digitally literate young people learn to ask: Who created this content? For what purpose was it produced? What interests or perspectives might be shaping it? Evaluating intent is as important as evaluating accuracy . Content may be factually correct while still being misleading if it omits context, emphasises emotional reactions or serves a hidden agenda. Learning to identify tone, framing and language helps young people understand how meaning is constructed in digital media."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#evidence-context-and-comparison-s2","t":"Evidence, context and comparison","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Fact-checking is most effective when approached as an ongoing process rather than a single action. Instead of searching for absolute certainty, young people benefit from comparing information across multiple sources and perspectives. This includes: checking whether claims are supported by evidence looking for corroboration from independent sources identifying missing voices or unexplored viewpoints Context plays a decisive role in this process. Images, statistics or quotes may change meaning when removed from their original context. Developing sensitivity to context helps young people recognise partial truths and avoid unintentionally spreading misleading information."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#ai-generated-content-and-verification-challenges-s2","t":"AI-generated content and verification challenges","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Recent developments in artificial intelligence have introduced new challenges to information evaluation. AI technologies are now capable of generating highly realistic images, videos, voices and texts that may appear authentic even when they are artificially created. As a result, visual realism alone can no longer be treated as proof of authenticity . A convincing video, audio message or photograph circulating online may be synthetic or manipulated through AI tools. For young people navigating digital platforms, this means that verification increasingly relies on analysing sources, context and cross- checking information rather than relying solely on visual credibility. Discussing AI- generated media in youth work settings helps young people understand how digital technologies can influence perception and credibility."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#managing-uncertainty-and-emotional-triggers-s2","t":"Managing uncertainty and emotional triggers","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Information evaluation also requires emotional awareness . Digital content often appeals to fear, outrage or urgency , encouraging immediate reactions. Fact-checking competence therefore includes the ability to recognise emotional triggers and delay judgement . Learning to say “I am not sure yet” is not a weakness but an important critical skill. By normalising uncertainty , youth workers can help young people resist the pressure to react instantly and instead value reflection and dialogue."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#fact-checking-as-a-shared-practice-s2","t":"Fact-checking as a shared practice","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Fact-checking should not be framed as an individual burden or a test of intelligence. In youth work settings, it is most effective when treated as a collective practice . Discussing information together, comparing interpretations and reflecting on different perspectives fosters confidence and mutual learning. This approach reinforces the idea that critical evaluation is not about distrusting everything, but about building informed trust through verification , comparison and openness to correction ."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#useful-fact-checking-resources-s2","t":"Useful fact-checking resources","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Youth workers may also introduce participants to accessible verification tools that support responsible information evaluation: Snopes – fact-checking viral claims and misinformation https://www.snopes.com EUvsDisinfo – European initiative analysing disinformation narratives https://euvsdisinfo.eu Google Reverse Image Search – verifying the origin of images https://images.google.com TinEye – reverse image search for detecting reused or manipulated visuals https://tineye.com These tools support critical thinking but do not replace it . The goal of digital literacy is not only to check information technically, but to develop habits of questioning, reflection and responsible sharing. Key message Information evaluation and fact-checking are not about finding a single correct answer. They are about developing habits of questioning, comparison and reflection that allow young people to navigate complexity without fear or disengagement. When young people learn to pause, ask the right questions and verify information collaboratively, digital spaces become environments for informed participation rather than confusion or manipulation."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#critical-thinking-and-online-safety-s2","t":"2.2. Critical thinking and online safety","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Critical thinking and online safety are closely interconnected competences . In digital environments, the ability to think critically directly influences how safely young people navigate platforms, relationships and information flows. Safety is not achieved through withdrawal or restriction , but through understanding how digital spaces operate and how individual choices shape exposure and risk. Critical thinking begins with awareness. Young people need opportunities to recognise that digital content, interactions and visibility are never neutral . Platforms are designed to capture attention, amplify emotion and reward speed rather than reflection. Without critical distance , safety risks increase-not because young people lack responsibility, but because digital environments often encourage impulsive engagement ."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#from-risk-awareness-to-risk-navigation-s2","t":"From risk awareness to risk navigation","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Online safety is often introduced through lists of dangers such as cyberbullying, scams, privacy violations or harmful content. While awareness of these risks is important, it is not sufficient on its own. Young people also need the ability to navigate risk-making informed decisions in complex situations rather than simply following rigid rules. Critical thinking supports this shift by encouraging young people to ask: What is happening in this situation? Who is involved, and what power dynamics might be present? What could be the consequences of my action-or my silence? These questions allow safety to become contextual and adaptive rather than fear-driven."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#identity-impersonation-and-digital-trust-s2","t":"Identity, impersonation and digital trust","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Another growing challenge in digital environments is identity misuse and impersonation. Online profiles, images, usernames and personal information can be copied, manipulated or falsely presented by others. In some cases, individuals may create fake accounts pretending to be someone else, while in other situations personal data or photos may be reused without consent. Critical thinking helps young people recognise potential signs of identity misuse, such as: unfamiliar accounts using known names or images suspicious requests for personal information messages that imitate trusted contacts or institutions manipulated profiles designed to gain trust or attention Understanding these risks helps young people develop cautious and responsible digital habits . Rather than assuming that every online identity is authentic, young people learn to verify information, question unexpected messages and protect their own digital identity . This awareness is especially important as digital technologies-including AI-generated images or voices-make identity imitation increasingly convincing. Developing critical awareness around digital identity therefore becomes an essential part of online safety education."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#understanding-visibility-and-consequences-s2","t":"Understanding visibility and consequences","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"A central aspect of online safety is understanding visibility. Digital actions - posting, commenting, sharing or reacting - can have effects far beyond the immediate moment or intended audience. Critical thinking helps young people reflect on: who might see this content now or in the future how content could be interpreted outside its original context whether consent and personal boundaries are respected This reflection supports safer participation without discouraging expression. Young people do not need to disappear from digital spaces to remain safe; they need tools to manage their presence consciously ."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#emotional-awareness-and-self-regulation-s2","t":"Emotional awareness and self-regulation","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Digital interactions are often emotionally charged. Content designed to provoke anger, fear or urgency can override critical judgement and lead to unsafe decisions. Critical thinking therefore includes emotional literacy : recognising when emotions influence reactions and learning to pause before responding . For youth work practice, this means creating environments where slowing down is valued. Choosing not to respond immediately, not to share unverified content or not to escalate online conflict is framed as an active and thoughtful decision rather than passivity."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#safety-as-a-collective-practice-s2","t":"Safety as a collective practice","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Online safety is not only an individual responsibility. Critical thinking supports collective safety by encouraging young people to consider how their actions affect others. This includes: intervening respectfully when peers are targeted online supporting individuals experiencing harassment using reporting and blocking tools without stigma contributing to shared norms of respectful digital interaction When safety is approached collectively, young people feel less isolated and more empowered to act responsibly."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#the-role-of-youth-work-s2","t":"The role of youth work","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"In youth work contexts, critical thinking and online safety should be integrated into everyday learning activities rather than taught as isolated topics. Facilitators play a key role by: encouraging dialogue instead of prescribing behaviour validating questions and uncertainty modelling reflective digital practices framing safety as part of participation rather than a restriction This approach strengthens young people’s agency and confidence while supporting responsible digital engagement. Key message Critical thinking transforms online safety from a list of warnings into a lived competence . When young people learn to reflect, question and regulate their digital actions, they become better equipped to remain visible, creative and engaged without exposing themselves or others to unnecessary harm. Online safety is strongest when it grows from understanding, dialogue and shared responsibility ."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#section-3-practical-tools-activities","t":"Section 3 · Practical tools & Activities","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":""},{"u":"chapter-1.html#digital-media-literacy-in-practice-s3","t":"3.1. Digital & media literacy in practice","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"This section provides a set of ready-to-use, low-threshold activities that youth workers and educators can directly implement in workshops, classrooms or informal learning settings. The activities translate digital and media literacy competences-such as information evaluation, critical thinking and online safety -into hands-on, reflective and participatory learning experiences. All activities are designed to be: Practice-based – learning by doing, not lecturing Safe-by-design – no exposure to real harm or personal risk Discussion-oriented – collective reflection rather than individual testing Adaptable – applicable across different European youth work contexts Educators and youth workers are also encouraged to complement these activities with national digital literacy resources , educational websites or local initiatives available in their country. This helps participants connect the workshop experience with reliable learning materials they can explore independently. 1 Stop, check, decide View activity PDF 2 Is this true? View activity PDF 3 My digital footprint, my choice View activity PDF 4 Inside the algorithm View activity PDF"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#practical-digital-safety-checklist-for-youth-s3","t":"3.2. Practical digital safety checklist for youth","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Everyday guide for safer online participation"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#purpose-of-the-tool-s3","t":"Purpose of the tool","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"This checklist supports young people in making informed, confident and responsible choices in digital environments. It focuses on everyday online situations and encourages awareness, reflection and responsible participation rather than fear or restriction . In this chapter, a short practical version of the checklist is provided to support workshop discussions and youth activities. The extended version of the checklist with detailed explanations and examples is available in the Annex Toolkit section of this guide. The full extended checklist with detailed guidance, examples and workshop exercises"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#how-to-use-this-checklist-in-youth-work-s3","t":"How to use this checklist in youth work","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Youth workers may use this checklist in several ways: as a quick discussion starter during digital literacy workshops as a reflection exercise at the end of an activity as a printed visual reminder for young participants as a starting point for creating group safety agreements as a co-creation activity where participants adapt, modify or rewrite the checklist based on their own digital experiences and needs The goal is not to present strict rules but to encourage reflection and responsible digital habits. Everyday guide for safer online participation Before sharing, reacting or posting online, take a moment to pause . Small online actions can have long-term consequences for privacy , reputation and well-being."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#personal-information-s3","t":"Personal information","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"I avoid sharing sensitive personal information such as my full name, address, school or phone number. I avoid posting my real-time location. I think about whether I would feel comfortable if my post stayed online for years."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#passwords-account-protection-s3","t":"Passwords & account protection","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"I use strong and unique passwords for my accounts. I activate two-factor authentication when possible. I avoid logging into my accounts on shared or public devices."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#responsible-sharing-s3","t":"Responsible sharing","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"I pause before sharing posts, videos or screenshots. I check whether the information might be misleading or harmful. I ask permission before sharing photos or personal information about others."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#recognising-online-risks-s3","t":"Recognising online risks","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"I am cautious when receiving suspicious links or unknown messages. I check profiles before trusting requests or information. I avoid sharing personal information with unknown accounts."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#digital-well-being-s3","t":"Digital well-being","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"I take breaks from screens when I feel overwhelmed. I pay attention to how online content affects my emotions. I try to balance online activities with offline life."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#final-reflection-s3","t":"Final reflection","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Which digital habit would you like to improve? What small step could help you make safer decisions online?"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#section-4-case-studies-best-practices","t":"Section 4 · Case studies & Best practices","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":""},{"u":"chapter-1.html#case-study-s4-1","t":"4.1. Case study 1 - Public media–led digital media literacy training","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"TRT Academy – Open-access digital media literacy trainings Source / Reference: https://www.trtakademi.com"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#project-overview-s4","t":"Project overview","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"The open-access digital media literacy courses offered by TRT Academy represent a public media–led initiative aimed at strengthening digital and media literacy in Türkiye through an accessible learning model . The trainings are designed to support safe, critical and responsible participation in digital environments and are available to a broad audience without financial or institutional barriers . Within these programmes, digital media literacy is not treated solely as a technical skill. Instead, it is approached as a multidimensional competence that combines information verification , algorithmic awareness, ethical responsibility, digital security and critical thinking . This holistic approach encourages learners to move beyond passive media consumption and engage with digital environments as responsible and reflective participants."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#objectives-and-learning-focus-s4","t":"Objectives and learning focus","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"The TRT Academy courses aim to: strengthen critical evaluation of information in digital environments raise awareness of misinformation , manipulation and algorithmic influence promote safe and responsible digital behaviours support informed participation in digital society Rather than focusing on specific platforms or tools, the programme emphasises transferable competences that remain relevant across different digital environments."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"The training combines conceptual explanations with examples drawn from everyday digital experiences. Complex topics such as misinformation, algorithms or digital security are translated into accessible language and real- life situations. Key thematic areas include: Information verification : questioning sources and analysing context Algorithmic awareness: understanding how platforms influence visibility and attention Digital security: managing personal data, privacy and digital footprints Ethics and responsibility: consent, representation and respectful online behaviour The pedagogical approach focuses on empowerment and informed decision- making rather than fear-based messaging ."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#target-group-s4","t":"Target group","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Young people, particularly those aged 16–30 Teachers, youth workers and educators Parents and adult learners seeking to better understand digital risks and opportunities Individuals who actively use digital media but lack structured critical awareness"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#key-digital-media-literacy-elements-s4","t":"Key digital & media literacy elements","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Critical evaluation and verification of online information Algorithmic awareness and recognition of hidden digital influences Ethical responsibility in content sharing and digital interaction Digital security, privacy awareness and well-being Understanding long-term implications of digital footprints"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#impact-s4","t":"Impact","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Publicly accessible initiatives such as TRT Academy contribute to: expanding digital media literacy beyond formal education settings encouraging more reflective engagement with online information strengthening resilience against misinformation and manipulation promoting responsible digital citizenship in everyday life Participants report increased awareness of how digital environments influence their perceptions and greater confidence in navigating online spaces responsibly."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#what-youth-workers-can-take-from-this-example-s4","t":"What youth workers can take from this example","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Youth workers can integrate similar approaches by combining accessible content with real-life examples and focusing on transferable skills rather than platform- specific knowledge. They can also design learning activities that connect critical thinking , digital safety and ethical awareness, helping young people apply these competences in everyday digital situations."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#case-study-s4-2","t":"4.2. Case study 2- Public media & youth participation","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"BBC Young Reporter – United Kingdom Source / Reference: https://www.bbc.co.uk/youngreporter"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#project-overview-s4-2","t":"Project overview","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"BBC Young Reporter is a long-standing public media initiative in the United Kingdom that combines youth participation, digital media literacy and journalistic practice. The programme enables young people to move from passive media consumption to active participation in media production, critical analysis and storytelling. Through collaboration with schools, youth organisations and community groups, the initiative provides young participants with opportunities to create journalistic content while learning how media systems operate."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#objectives-and-learning-focus-s4-2","t":"Objectives and learning focus","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"The programme aims to: develop young people’s understanding of media production processes strengthen skills in fact-checking and source evaluation promote responsible and ethical storytelling support youth participation in public dialogue build confidence in expressing ideas and experiences"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4-2","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"BBC Young Reporter follows a learning-by-doing model , where media literacy is developed through real production experiences rather than theoretical instruction. Activities typically include: youth-led reporting and interviews digital storytelling projects editorial mentoring by media professionals fact-checking and verification processes group reflection on representation, tone and media ethics This approach positions media literacy as a practical competence connected to participation, creativity and responsibility ."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#target-group-s4-2","t":"Target group","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Young people aged approximately 11–18 (with adapted formats for older youth) Young participants from diverse social, cultural and educational backgrounds Educators and youth workers acting as facilitators and mentors"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#key-digital-media-literacy-elements-s4-2","t":"Key digital & media literacy elements","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Information verification and responsible journalism Understanding media framing and narrative construction Ethical use of images, language and representation Digital safety, consent and online responsibility Awareness of audience, visibility and public impact"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#impact-s4-2","t":"Impact","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Participation in BBC Young Reporter helps young people: understand how media content is produced and verified develop confidence in expressing opinions and stories build critical distance from misinformation and sensational narratives gain visibility within a trusted public media environment Such initiatives demonstrate how youth media production can strengthen both critical media awareness and civic participation ."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#what-youth-workers-can-take-from-this-example-s4-2","t":"What youth workers can take from this example","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Youth workers can encourage media literacy through hands-on content creation , such as digital storytelling or youth reporting activities. By allowing young people to produce and reflect on their own content, facilitators can strengthen both critical awareness and confidence in expression ."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#case-study-s4-3","t":"4.3. Case study 3- Civil society–led media literacy initiative","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Lie Detectors – European Media Literacy Initiative Source / Reference: https://lie-detectors.org"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#project-overview-s4-3","t":"Project overview","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Lie Detectors is a European civil society initiative that promotes media literacy and critical thinking among young people through direct interaction with professional journalists . Operating across several European countries, the programme connects schools and youth groups with journalists who facilitate workshops on misinformation , verification and responsible media consumption. The initiative focuses on helping young people understand how journalism works, how misinformation spreads , and how to verify information in a rapidly evolving digital media landscape. By combining professional expertise with participatory learning methods , Lie Detectors provides a practical approach to media literacy education."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#objectives-and-learning-focus-s4-3","t":"Objectives and learning focus","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"The programme aims to: strengthen young people’s ability to recognise misinformation and manipulation introduce basic fact-checking and verification techniques explain how journalism works and why credible information matters encourage critical questioning of online content promote responsible participation in digital information environments"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#methodological-approach-s4","t":"Methodological approach","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Lie Detectors workshops are typically delivered through interactive sessions led by trained journalists. Rather than relying on lectures, the methodology emphasises discussion, analysis and real-life examples from contemporary media environments. Key elements of the workshops include: analysing viral news stories and misleading headlines learning how journalists verify sources and information discussing the role of algorithms and social media in information circulation exploring the difference between opinion, reporting and misinformation reflecting on the responsibilities of both media producers and audiences Participants are encouraged to question information collaboratively and understand media systems as dynamic environments shaped by both technology and human decisions."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#target-groups-s4","t":"Target groups","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Students and young people aged approximately 12–18 Teachers and youth workers facilitating media literacy activities Schools and youth organisations across multiple European countries"},{"u":"chapter-1.html#impact-s4-3","t":"Impact","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Initiatives such as Lie Detectors demonstrate how cooperation between journalism professionals and educators can strengthen young people’s media literacy skills. Participants often report: greater confidence in questioning online information improved understanding of how news is produced stronger awareness of misinformation strategies increased trust in credible information sources By bridging journalism and education, the programme contributes to developing critical, informed and responsible media users ."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#what-youth-workers-can-take-from-this-example-s4-3","t":"What youth workers can take from this example","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"Youth workers can collaborate with external experts or use real-world examples to make media literacy more concrete and credible. Inviting professionals or simulating journalistic practices can help young people better understand how information is produced and verified."},{"u":"chapter-1.html#key-takeaways-for-youth-work-practice-s4","t":"4.4. Key takeaways for youth work practice","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":"The case studies presented in this chapter demonstrate that digital and media literacy is most effective when it is embedded in real practices rather than abstract explanations. Initiatives led by public media and civil society actors show how young people can engage with media as both critical users and active creators . Across these examples, several key approaches emerge for youth work practice: connecting media literacy with real-world media practices and professional standards encouraging dialogue, questioning and collaborative analysis instead of fixed answers supporting young people in becoming both critical media consumers and responsible content creators creating safe environments where reflection, experimentation and participation are possible These approaches align with youth work principles that prioritise participation, creativity and critical engagement. They also connect directly with the OURCULTURE methodology, where digital literacy supports cultural exploration, storytelling and youth-led narratives. By combining critical awareness with creative expression , young people are better equipped to explore their cultural environments, document local heritage and share meaningful digital stories. The following chapters build on this foundation, moving from media analysis toward cultural exploration, heritage documentation and youth-led digital storytelling. ← Back Home Next → Ch. 2 · Local Cultural Documentation"},{"u":"chapter-1-activity-1.html","t":"Activity 1 · Stop, check, decide","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":" Material list 1.Increased awareness of emotional and algorithmic triggers 2.Practical questioning habits for everyday digital use 3.Stronger link between critical thinking and online safety 4.Shared language for responsible digital participation 5.Greater confidence in choosing not to react immediately Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Stop, check, decide Reference Media literacy and digital citizenship approaches focusing on critical reflection, source evaluation and responsible online behaviour. (Designed as an original youth work activity; adaptable to different national and local contexts.) Printed or digital examples of online content (posts, headlines, screenshots, short messages) - realistic but anonymised or fictionalised Flipchart or whiteboard Markers or sticky notes Timer or phone (optional) Overview Minimum: 45 minutes Recommended: 70 minutes Extended: 90 minutes with deeper discussion For participants: What did I learn about my own digital habits? Which question helped me most to think differently? How can pausing improve my online safety? For youth workers: Did participants feel safe to express uncertainty? Were multiple perspectives respected? Did the activity encourage reflection rather than rule- following? Learning objectives This activity helps young people slow down their digital reactions and develop a habit of critical reflection before sharing, commenting or engaging with online content. Through real-life examples, participants practise questioning information, recognising emotional triggers and making safer, more conscious decisions in digital environments. By the end of the activity, participants will be able to: 1.recognise situations where misinformation or manipulation may occur 2.apply simple critical questions before reacting online 3.understand the link between critical thinking and online safety 4.reflect on emotional triggers in digital content 5.make more conscious choices about sharing and engagement. Warm-up: Fast reactions (~10 minutes) The facilitator ask the young people to share personal short examples of viral content or sensational headlines that they have come across or may have shared themselves. Introducing the “Stop, check, decide” model (~10 minutes) The facilitator introduces a simple three-step model: STOP Pause before reacting. Notice emotions (anger, fear, excitement). CHECK Ask critical questions: Who created this? Why was it shared? What might be missing? Is it trying to inform or provoke? DECIDE Choose consciously: Share / comment / report / ignore Or wait and verify This model is written visibly for everyone. Group work: Applying the model (~20–25 minutes) Participants work in small groups (3–5 people). Each group receives 1–2 content examples. They discuss: What is our first reaction? What changes when we STOP? What questions help us CHECK? What is the safest and most responsible DECISION? The facilitator moves between groups, asking guiding questions but not correcting answers. Collective Discussion (~15 minutes) Groups share insights: What surprised you? Did anyone change their initial reaction? Which questions were most helpful? How does this relate to online safety? The focus is on reflection, not judgement. Transfer to real life (~10 minutes) Participants reflect individually or in pairs: Where could I use this model in my daily digital life? What makes it difficult to pause online? How could peers support each other to slow down? & Methodology Steps: 27 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 70 min. "},{"u":"chapter-1-activity-2.html","t":"Activity 2 · Is this true?","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":" Material list 1.Increased awareness of misinformation patterns 2.Improved ability to question content before sharing 3.Reduced pressure to react immediately 4.Shared understanding that verification is a collective process 5.Stronger confidence in critical judgement Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Is this true? Reference Media literacy approaches focusing on source verification, critical questioning and responsible information sharing. (Original activity design for youth work and non-formal education contexts.) Printed or digital news-style examples (headlines, posts, screenshots): 1. One reliable source 2. One misleading or manipulated example 3. One ambiguous or unclear example Internet access (optional, for extended version) Flipchart or whiteboard Markers or sticky notes Overview Minimum: 60 minutes with fewer examples Recommended: 75 minutes Extended: 90 minutes with verification tools For participants: What made this activity challenging? Which content was hardest to judge - and why? How can I avoid spreading misinformation unintentionally? For youth workers: Did participants feel comfortable expressing doubt? Were discussions focused on reasoning rather than guessing? Did the activity promote shared responsibility? Learning objectives This activity supports young people in recognising misinformation and fake news by analysing realistic digital content. Instead of memorising rules, participants learn to question sources, intentions and context through practical comparison and discussion. The activity strengthens confidence in saying “I’m not sure yet” and promotes verification as a normal part of digital participation. By the end of the activity, participants will be able to: 1.identify common characteristics of misinformation and fake news 2.distinguish between fact, opinion and manipulation 3.apply basic verification steps before sharing content 4.understand how emotions and speed influence belief 5.develop collective verification habits. Warm-up: First impressions (~10 minutes) Participants are shown several content examples. They respond individually: Do I believe this? Would I share it? Why or why not? No answers are labelled as right or wrong. What makes information trustworthy? (~10 minutes) The facilitator asks: What makes you trust a piece of information? What makes you suspicious? Responses are written visibly and grouped (source, language, visuals, emotions, context). Group Investigation (~25–30 minutes) Participants work in small groups (3–5 people). Each group analyses one content example using guiding questions: Who created this? Is the source visible and identifiable? What evidence is provided? What emotions does it trigger? Is anything missing or exaggerated? Groups label the content as: Likely reliable Unclear / needs verification Likely misleading The focus is on reasoning, not certainty. Comparing and discussing results (~15–20 minutes) Groups present their conclusions and explain: What clues influenced their judgement? Where did doubts remain? The facilitator highlights that uncertainty is part of media literacy, not a failure. Simple verification tools (optional, 10–15 minutes) If internet access is available, the facilitator introduces basic verification actions: checking the original source, searching the headline or image, comparing with other trusted outlets. This step is exploratory, not technical. Methodology Steps: & 28 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 75 min. "},{"u":"chapter-1-activity-3.html","t":"Activity 3 · My digital footprint, my choice","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":" Material list 1.Increased awareness of digital footprints 2.Better understanding of privacy and consent 3.Practical strategies for safer online behaviour 4.Reduced fear and increased sense of control 5.Stronger link between safety and responsibility Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection My digital footprint, my choice Reference Non-formal education approaches to online safety, digital wellbeing and responsible participation. (Original activity design for youth work and youth groups.) Printed scenario cards (or slides) Flipchart or whiteboard Markers / sticky notes Paper and pens for individual reflection Overview Minimum: 60 minutes with fewer scenarios Recommended: 70 minutes Extended: 90 minutes For participants: What surprised you about digital footprints? Which situations felt most risky - and why? How can safety be proactive, not reactive? For youth workers: Did participants see safety as empowerment? Were discussions grounded in real-life behaviour? Did the activity support reflective decision-making? Learning objectives This activity helps young people understand that every digital action leaves traces and that online safety is not about fear or restriction, but about informed choices. Through reflection and scenario-based discussion, participants explore how digital footprints are created and how they can manage visibility, privacy and responsibility in digital spaces. The focus is on empowerment rather than control. By the end of the activity, participants will be able to: 1.understand what a digital footprint is and how it is created 2.recognise short- and long-term consequences of online actions 3.identify basic online safety risks 4.reflect on privacy, consent and visibility 5.make more conscious decisions about sharing and interaction online. Warm-up: What do we leave behind? (~10 minutes) The facilitator asks participants: What do you leave behind when you use social media? What happens to posts, comments or photos over time? Answers are collected visually to introduce the idea of a digital footprint. Digital footprint scenarios (~20 minutes) Participants are divided into small groups. Each group receives one scenario, for example: sharing a photo from a private event, posting a comment in anger, accepting an unknown follower, using the same password on multiple platforms. Groups discuss: What digital traces are created? Who might see this now? Who could see it later? Risk or Choice? (~20 minutes) Groups categorise elements of the scenario as: Low risk Medium risk High risk The facilitator emphasises that risk depends on context, audience and intention, not only on the action itself. Safety by Design ((~15–20 minutes) Groups redesign the scenario: What could be done differently? How could privacy or safety be improved? What questions should be asked before acting? Groups share one improved version with the whole group. Personal reflection: My one rule (~10 minutes) Each participant completes the sentence: “One thing I will be more careful about online is…” This step supports transfer to everyday practice. Methodology Steps: & 29 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 70 min. "},{"u":"chapter-1-activity-4.html","t":"Activity 4 · Inside the algorithm","c":"Chapter 1 · Digital & Media Literacy","x":" Material list 1.Clear understanding of algorithmic influence 2.Awareness of filter bubbles and echo chambers 3.Practical strategies for digital diversification 4.Reduced sense of helplessness 5.Increased critical distance from feeds Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Inside the algorithm Reference Non-formal education approaches to algorithmic awareness, critical media literacy and digital citizenship. (Original activity design for youth work and youth groups.) Sticky notes (two colours) Flipchart or whiteboard Markers Scenario cards or short prompts Optional: smartphones (not mandatory) Overview Recommended: 80 minutes Extended: 1 h 40 min with longer discussions For participants: How much choice do we really have online? What behaviours feed algorithms the most? What small changes could shift your digital environment? For youth workers: Did participants relate algorithms to daily habits? Was fear replaced by curiosity and agency? Did the activity encourage critical reflection? Learning objectives This activity helps young people understand how algorithms shape what they see, hear and engage with online. Rather than treating algorithms as invisible or unavoidable forces, the activity frames them as systems that can be understood, questioned and partially influenced through conscious behaviour. Participants explore filter bubbles, echo chambers and personalisation through concrete examples from their own digital lives. By the end of the activity, participants will be able to: 1.explain what algorithms do in simple terms 2.recognise filter bubbles and echo chambers 3.understand how interaction patterns affect content visibility 4.reflect on how algorithms influence opinions and emotions 5.identify strategies to diversify their digital experience. Warm-up: Why do I see this? (~10 minutes) Participants are asked: Why do you think this post or video appeared on your screen? Have you ever wondered why some content never appears? Responses are noted to introduce algorithmic selection. How algorithms learn (~15 minutes) The facilitator explains, in accessible language: likes, clicks, comments and watch time, repetition and prediction, “more of the same” logic. No technical detail is required-only patterns. Building a filter bubble (~20 minutes) Participants write: on one colour: topics they often like or watch, on another colour: topics they rarely see. Notes are placed on a wall to visualise visibility vs. invisibility. Group discussion: What becomes dominant? What disappears? Echo chamber scenarios (~20 minutes) Small groups receive scenarios, such as: only following accounts with similar opinions, reacting strongly to emotional content, unfollowing dissenting voices. Groups discuss: How does this affect perspective? What happens to dialogue? Escaping the Bubble (15–20 minutes) Groups design “algorithm-aware behaviours”, for example: following diverse sources, pausing before reacting, actively searching opposing views, limiting passive scrolling. Groups share one strategy each. Methodology Steps: & 30 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 80 min. "},{"u":"chapter-2.html#infographic-summary","t":"I N F O G R A P H I C S U M M A R Y","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Visual summary of this chapter — click to enlarge or download. Download infographic (PDF)"},{"u":"chapter-2.html#intro","t":"Introduction","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"After developing critical digital and media literacy skills in Chapter 1, the next step is to explore the cultural environments in which young people live. Before young people can create meaningful stories about their communities, they first need to learn how to observe, question and document cultural realities around them. This chapter therefore focuses on cultural exploration and documentation . It provides youth workers with practical tools to help young people: understand what culture means in everyday life identify cultural topics worth exploring collect reliable cultural information from different sources learn basic investigation methods inspired by ethnography and oral history The activities encourage curiosity, observation and dialogue with local communities. Through these processes, young people become active explorers of their own cultural environment."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#digcomp-3-0-connection-sintro","t":"DigComp 3.0 connection","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"This chapter mainly supports Information search, evaluation and management, helping young people collect, assess and organise cultural information from different sources."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#learning-objectives-sintro","t":"Learning objectives","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"By the end of this chapter, youth workers will be able to: help young people reflect on the meaning of culture in everyday life guide young participants in identifying cultural topics to explore support young people in identifying different types of sources facilitate cultural investigation through interviews , observation and documentation prepare young participants to transform collected material into stories (Chapter 3). Sections Section 1 | Why local culture matters? Section 2 | Cultural documentation methods Section 3 | Practical tools & activities Section 4 | Case studies & best practices"},{"u":"chapter-2.html#section-1-why-local-culture-matters","t":"Section 1 · Why local culture matters?","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":""},{"u":"chapter-2.html#introduction-to-the-theme-of-culture-s1","t":"Introduction to the theme of culture","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":""},{"u":"chapter-2.html#understanding-culture-in-everyday-life-s1","t":"Understanding culture in everyday life","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"When young people hear the word culture, they often think first about museums, monuments or famous works of art. However, culture is much broader . It includes the ways people think, act, communicate and live together. Culture can be understood as: shared habits and traditions ways of speaking and expressing emotions food practices and celebrations music, clothing and everyday creativity social norms and collective memories In sociology and anthropology, culture is generally understood as the set of practices, values and meanings shared by a group of people and transmitted through social life. This perspective emphasises that culture is learned, shared and evolving . Young people are therefore not only recipients of culture - they are also participants who transform it ."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#culture-as-layers-the-russian-doll-approach-s1","t":"Culture as layers: The “Russian doll” approach","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"One way to understand culture is to imagine it as a set of layers , similar to Russian nesting dolls. Each layer represents a different stage of socialisation that shapes who we are. For example: Inner layer – family influences (language, traditions, food, values) Second layer – school and education (norms, knowledge, expectations) Third layer – friends and peer groups (music, styles, shared references) Fourth layer – digital culture and global influences (social media, online communities) Outer layer – personal choices and identity Through these layers, young people realise that their identity is shaped by many influences and experiences . This approach is inspired by sociological perspectives such as those of Pierre Bourdieu, who showed how individuals develop cultural dispositions through different forms of socialisation. Understanding these layers helps young people recognise that culture is not fixed . It evolves through interaction, learning and creativity."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#connecting-young-people-to-cultural-heritage-s1","t":"Connecting young people to cultural heritage","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Local culture also connects young people to history and collective memory . Exploring the past helps young people understand: where their community comes from how traditions have evolved how social changes influence cultural practices However, heritage is not only found in museums or official archives. It also exists in: family stories neighbourhood memories everyday objects community practices oral histories Key point: Young people can therefore become collectors of culture , documenting stories that might otherwise disappear."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#culture-and-civic-engagement-s1","t":"Culture and civic engagement","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Understanding culture and documenting local culture can also strengthen civic engagement. When young people explore their cultural environment, they begin to ask questions such as: What stories are told about our community? Whose voices are missing? What traditions are changing? What should be preserved or transformed? When young people explore cultural practices, collect stories or highlight community experiences, they create cultural narratives that circulate in digital environments. This digital topic is explored further in Chapter 5, where young people learn how responsible digital participation can support dialogue, representation and community engagement. This process strengthens both identity and dialogue with others."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#section-2-cultural-documentation-methods","t":"Section 2 · Cultural documentation methods","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Three ways to document culture: interview people, observe cultural environments, and explore existing sources. Once a cultural topic has been identified, the next step is to collect information. This process can be approached as a form of cultural investigation , inspired by ethnographic research methods. Cultural documentation also requires critical awareness : questioning sources , recognising biases and reflecting on how our own perspective influences what we observe. Youth workers do not need to become professional researchers. However, simple investigation methods can help young people explore culture more deeply. Three key approaches are particularly useful."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#tools-s2","t":"Tools","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"A notebook and a pen to start, plus the Ethno’teens tools. Digital tools can then be used (see Chapter 4)."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#interviews-and-oral-history-s2","t":"Interviews and oral history","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"One of the richest ways to explore culture is by talking to people. Interviews allow young people to collect stories, memories and personal experiences. Possible interview partners include: family members older residents artisans or shop owners community leaders cultural practitioners Good interviews rely on: open questions attentive listening curiosity about personal experiences Examples of open questions: Who taught you this tradition? When did you first learn it? What does it mean to you? When is it usually practised? Through interviews , young people learn that culture is often transmitted through stories and experiences ."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#observation-s2","t":"Observation","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Observation means paying attention to what happens in everyday environments. Young people can observe: public spaces community events markets, streets or neighbourhoods cultural practices in daily life"},{"u":"chapter-2.html#the-key-principle-is-simple-observe-before-judging-s2","t":"The key principle is simple: observe before judging.","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Participants can practise describing what they see as precisely as possible before interpreting it. Key message Observe without judging. Set aside judgement and embrace curiosity."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#cultural-archives-and-sources-s2","t":"Cultural archives and sources","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Cultural information can also be found in many types of sources. These may include: Institutional sources local archives museums libraries municipal records Informal sources family photo albums personal collections neighbourhood stories community memories"},{"u":"chapter-2.html#digital-sources-s2","t":"Digital sources","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"online archives digital mapping platforms social media communities Combining different types of sources helps young people build a richer understanding of their cultural topic ."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#organising-collected-information-s2","t":"Organising collected information","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Once information is collected, it must be organised . Participants can learn simple practices such as: dating interviews and observations noting locations and contexts assigning keywords to recordings or photos creating folders for different types of materials Collect with purpose : keep what is meaningful and useful rather than accumulating unnecessary digital material. This step prepares the collected material for storytelling and media production, which will be explored in later chapters. Key messages Unprocessed information is of no use. Misplaced information is lost. Too much information is counterproductive."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#section-3-practical-tools-activities","t":"Section 3 · Practical tools & activities","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Workshop plan for youth-led local cultural projects"},{"u":"chapter-2.html#understanding-culture-activities-14-s3","t":"Understanding culture (Activities 1–4)","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"The following activities help youth workers transform cultural exploration into concrete learning experiences . All activities follow the same principles: practice-based learning collective discussion curiosity and open dialogue respect for different perspectives Participants are encouraged to collect information in a responsible and purposeful way , avoiding unnecessary data accumulation and focusing on meaningful documentation . 1 Heuristic card View activity PDF 2 The cultural iceberg View activity PDF 3 Collecting cultural memories through interviews View activity PDF 4 Cultural Layers - Understanding Identity Through the “Russian Doll” Model View activity PDF 5 Exploring culture through people, places and time View activity PDF 6 Exploring cultural communities online View activity PDF 7 At the table View activity PDF 8 Interview circle: Collecting cultural heritage stories View activity PDF"},{"u":"chapter-2.html#section-4-case-study","t":"Section 4 · Case study","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Workshop plan for youth-led local cultural projects"},{"u":"chapter-2.html#case-study-s4-1","t":"4.1. Case study 1 - Boîte à histoire (Storybox)","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Source / Reference : https://www.jeveuxaider.gouv.fr/organisations/13484-boite-a-histoires"},{"u":"chapter-2.html#project-overview-s4","t":"Project overview","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Boîte à Histoires is a non-profit organisation that supports families, care homes, businesses and local authorities in preserving their historical heritage . Boîte à Histoires is committed to collecting memories and passing them on from one generation to the next. The organisation’s members firmly believe that passing on memories and stories from the past helps us understand the present and move forward more effectively into the future. For them, the link between generations is forged by creating platforms that collect everyone’s memories and presenting them in the form of a book, a film or an exhibition. The project relies primarily on human resources, with young volunteers playing a key role. Two young people from the young workers’ hostel will lead the workshops and collect the stories, whilst other young volunteers will handle the editing."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#objective-and-learning-focus-s4","t":"Objective and learning focus","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Boîte à Histoires works to promote healthy ageing by fostering intergenerational connections . Its mission is to create spaces for dialogue and sharing between generations, enabling older people to play an active role in everyday life . The challenge lies in ensuring that young volunteers remain committed and available over the long term. They sometimes find a job and no longer have the time to continue. In this case, two young people took part in the project over the long term, gathering information and engaging with older people."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#activities-and-outputs-s4","t":"Activities and outputs","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"The non-profit organisation is calling for volunteers to help with its initiatives to collect memories and stories. The non-profit organisation France Bénévolat is seeking partners to take part in the project. The young workers’ hostel, which provides accommodation for young students and workers and helps them settle into local life in Paris, is taking part in the project. In the project described here, two young people from the youth workers’ hostel and a facilitator from the Boîte à histoires association visited a local care home four times to chat with the elderly residents. Another group of young volunteers will edit the footage, and a screening for families will take place at the care home."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"The young people filmed interviews with older people . Discussions were held on the themes of love, friendship and travel. These exchanges provided an opportunity to appreciate the differences between the lifestyles of different generations, changes in language, eating habits (potatoes formed the basis of meals), the social status of couples, and the impossibility of divorce. “The women recounted how they had often followed their husbands to their various work postings. They also spoke of their lives during the war. They described the Paris of the past, with its cobbled streets.” These exchanges are described as a source of inspiration for the young people."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#target-group-s4","t":"Target group","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Young people from 18 and old people of the area living in institutions."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#key-digital-media-literacy-elements-s4","t":"Key digital & media literacy elements","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"The activity requires the ability to listen and ask questions . The young people are supported by a member of the association during the workshops at the care home. Some technical tips on filming are shared. The footage is recorded using mobile phones . Tips : people should speak one after the other to ensure the audio is usable. The person filming should stabilise the phone by tucking their arms in close to their body and resting the phone near their chest to avoid shaking. Only those who have given their signed consent may be filmed. The young people must therefore be vigilant and not film anyone who has not given their consent."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#impact-s4","t":"Impact","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Older people often say that what they have to say isn’t interesting. Young people encourage them to share their stories and give value to their words by showcasing them in projects such as video montages that will be shared with their families. “Older people can sometimes be a bit shy, so we sometimes have to encourage them to speak. For these people, their lives feel as though they’re already coming to an end; everything around them reminds them of that, even though they have so much to share.”"},{"u":"chapter-2.html#why-it-matters-for-ourculture-s4","t":"Why it matters for Ourculture","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"This use case is inspiring for several reasons: by encouraging active listening and questioning , it enables the sharing of valuable insights from the past that inspire young people. These insights help them put their own experiences into perspective. Recording and sharing these testimonies helps to preserve the memories of older generations ."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#case-study-s4-2","t":"4.2. Case study 2 - Pocket Films - Empathie","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Source / Reference: https://www.pocketfilms-empathie.com"},{"u":"chapter-2.html#overview-of-the-project-s4","t":"Overview of the project","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"What is a ‘Pocket film’? “Pocket” as in your pocket. And ‘film’? Not as in film stock, but as in digital. So, digital in your pocket means a mobile phone. Films made on a mobile phone. But for whom and why? First and foremost for oneself, because looking at the world through a screen changes how we perceive it, and even more so when we enjoy telling the story of what we see. A Pocket film is a narrative supported by images , and images enhanced by the narrative, whether created alone or with others. A way of rekindling both an empathetic view of one’s own experiences of the world and of how others perceive it. And sometimes an opportunity to make a fresh start. “Pocket film empathy” is part of the “Petits laboratoires d’empathie” Federation (https://sergetisseron.com/petits-laboratoires-dempathie/)."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#objectives-and-activities-s4","t":"Objectives and activities","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Using images created together to develop both individual expression and an awareness of others . In practice, the Pocket Film project trains street workers to use mobile phones as a means of self-expression . It has two objectives: the first is to enable personal expression of the rage, anger and shame that often dwell within these young people, and to help them discover various facets of themselves in order to reduce the risk of identifying with a predefined role. The second is to enable them to discover that their perspectives differ from those of others, that they can be valued equally, and that they are even complementary."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4-2","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"The teenagers are first asked to talk about everything they do with their phones: mostly selfies... The facilitator also talks about what he does with his mobile phone. He then suggests they make a short film, in the form of a single- take sequence, lasting one to two minutes. “Take any object, film it and give it a voice .” The young people write their story beforehand if they wish. They begin to open up. It becomes possible to talk about anger, rage, shame or the difficulties of life by attributing these emotions to an object that speaks for itself: such as a shoe, a bin, a pen, a tissue… The bin can no longer stand everyone dumping their rubbish in it, the tissue that people use and throw away without a second thought, the shoe that is worn everywhere in all weathers without ever being thanked, and the pen that is never allowed to say what it wants. Target Groups: street workers and young people in difficulty. Impact: Making a Pocket film means turning the phone in our pocket into a creative tool , allowing us to reconnect with the expression of emotions, and then with the spoken word."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#what-youth-workers-can-take-from-this-example-s4","t":"What youth workers can take from this example","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"This project is inspiring because it creates stories using materials that are accessible to everyone . The smartphone, an everyday device usually used for consuming content, becomes a tool for exploration and self-expression through the workshops on offer. What matters is storytelling and the ability to express oneself, which is developed through this approach. This mastery of storytelling enables participants to discover and share unexpected treasures."},{"u":"chapter-2.html#from-cultural-exploration-to-cultural-storytelling-s4","t":"… from cultural exploration to cultural storytelling","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"Through the activities presented in this chapter, young people begin to explore culture in a concrete and participatory way . By reflecting on their own cultural influences, observing everyday practices, engaging in conversations with others and identifying different sources of information, participants progressively develop the ability to look at culture with curiosity and attention . These activities show that culture is not only found in official institutions such as museums or archives. It also exists in everyday life: in food traditions, family stories, neighbourhood memories, social practices and community experiences. The case studies presented above illustrate how youth-led cultural exploration projects can transform local stories and experiences into meaningful cultural initiatives. They demonstrate that young people can actively participate in documenting and preserving cultural knowledge within their communities. At this stage, participants have already begun to collect valuable cultural material: personal reflections about cultural identity stories gathered through interviews observations from everyday environments insights discovered through digital exploration Not all collected material becomes a story . The next step is to select, structure and give meaning to these elements, transform this material into engaging narratives that can be shared with others. In the following chapter, we will explore how these collected experiences and cultural insights can be developed into cultural storytelling projects, using creative methods and digital tools to share stories with wider audiences. ← Back Ch. 1 · Digital & Media Literacy Next → Ch. 3 · Cultural Storytelling"},{"u":"chapter-2-activity-1.html","t":"Activity 1 · Heuristic card","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":" Material list 1.A collective mind map illustrating participants’ different understandings of culture. 2.A selection of cultural topics that participants would like to explore further. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Heuristic card Reference Approaches to exploring the theme of culture that focus on group discussion and an openness to the diversity of sources within the young people’s immediate environment. This activity helps young people to realise the wealth of resources available to them, beyond the institutional resources offered by museums. (Designed as an original youth work activity; adaptable to different national and local contexts.) Colour markers Sticky notes (optional) Timer or phone (optional) Overview Minimum: 40 minutes Extended: 60 minutes with deeper discussion For participants: What surprised me during this activity? What cultural aspects had I never thought about before? For youth workers: What new ideas about culture might have emerged from the workshop and the discussions? Learning objectives This activity uses a collaborative mind mapping method, similar to techniques used in focus groups. Participants are invited to collectively answer the question: “What is culture?” Instead of giving a predefined definition, the facilitator encourages participants to explore their own understanding of culture. The goal is to broaden the participants’ perspective and help them realise that culture includes: • traditions and heritage • everyday practices • food and celebrations • music, language and expressions • social behaviours and values • youth cultures and subcultures By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.reflect on the meaning of culture in everyday life 2.understand that culture goes beyond museums and traditions 3.identify possible cultural topics to explore for their project. Introduction (10 minutes) The facilitator writes the word “Culture” in the centre of the large sheet of paper. Participants are asked the following open question: “When you hear the word culture, what comes to your mind?” Participants are invited to write words, draw symbols, or add examples around the central word. Important instruction from the facilitator: There are no right or wrong answers. The goal is to explore ideas freely. Expanding the map (15 minutes) Once the first ideas appear, the facilitator encourages participants to expand the map by asking follow-up questions such as: • Does culture only exist in museums? • Can food be part of culture? • What about music or clothing styles? • Are youth cultures part of culture? • Can culture change over time? Participants continue adding ideas, creating branches and connections on the mind map. They are encouraged to look at what others have written and build on those ideas. Identifying cultural topics (10–15 minutes) Once the mind map becomes richer, the facilitator invites participants to identify possible topics they could explore in their cultural project. Examples may include: • traditional recipes • neighbourhood stories • local festivals • youth music scenes • migration histories • everyday cultural practices Participants circle or highlight ideas that could become topics for further exploration in the project. Methodology Steps: 52 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 40 min. "},{"u":"chapter-2-activity-2.html","t":"Activity 2 · The cultural iceberg","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":" Material list 1.A broader definition of the concept of culture. 2.This is the stage at which young people first come up with ideas about which aspect of culture they would like to investigate and share. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection The cultural iceberg Reference Approaches to exploring the theme of culture that focus on group discussion and an openness to the diversity of sources within the young people’s immediate environment. This activity helps young people to realise the wealth of resources available to them, beyond the institutional resources offered by museums. (Designed as an original youth work activity; adaptable to different national and local contexts.) Printed iceberg templates (A3 format) Pens or coloured pencils Scissors (optional) Pre-prepared cards with cultural elements (optional) Overview Recommended: 1.5 hours Extended: 2 hours with a cultural portrait For participants: What surprised me during this activity? What cultural aspects had I never thought about before? For youth workers: What new ideas about culture might have emerged from the workshop and the discussions? Learning objectives This activity introduces the idea that culture can be compared to an iceberg. Just like an iceberg, culture has: Visible elements, things we immediately notice when encountering a culture. Invisible elements, beliefs, values and norms that influence behaviours but are not immediately obvious. Understanding this distinction helps young people realise why cultural misunderstandings sometimes occur. The activity also encourages participants to reflect on their own cultural layers. Understanding visible and invisible culture By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.understand that culture includes both visible and invisible elements 2.recognise that cultural behaviours and practices are shaped by deeper values and beliefs 3.reflect on their own cultural background and influences 4.develop empathy and openness towards cultural differences. This activity helps participants realise that what we see in culture is often only a small visible part of a much deeper reality. Introduction: Visible and invisible culture (~10 minutes) The facilitator introduces the iceberg metaphor. Participants are asked to imagine an iceberg where only a small part is visible above the water, while most of it remains hidden below the surface. The facilitator explains that culture works in a similar way. Examples can be discussed in plenary: Visible cultural elements: food, clothing, language Invisible cultural elements: values, beliefs, norms of behaviour Participants are invited to reflect on how both dimensions influence everyday interactions. Exploring Cultural Elements (~20 minutes preparation + 30 minutes sharing) Participants work in pairs or small groups. They receive cards containing different cultural elements (or write their own examples). They must decide whether each element belongs: above the iceberg (visible culture), below the iceberg (invisible culture) Examples may include: greeting styles, punctuality, family roles Groups place the cards on the iceberg template and prepare to explain their choices. Each group then briefly presents their iceberg to the rest of the participants. Reflecting on personal culture (~20 minutes) Participants now create their own cultural iceberg. They reflect individually on their personal cultural influences. Above the water they may write: foods they associate with their culture, languages they speak, celebrations or traditions Below the water they may include: values they learned growing up, family expectations, attitudes towards education or work, beliefs about community or identity. Participants are encouraged to take time to think about how their experiences shaped these elements. Cultural “portrait” exercise (optional) If time allows, participants can extend the activity by creating a cultural portrait. They imagine a fictional person from their cultural environment and describe: visible cultural traits, invisible cultural values, behaviours influenced by these values. This step helps participants understand how culture influences identity and social interactions. Methodology Steps: 53 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 90 min. "},{"u":"chapter-2-activity-3.html","t":"Activity 3 · Collecting cultural memories through interviews","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":"1.A set of open-ended interview questions. 2.Improved interviewing and active listening skills. 3.Greater awareness of how personal memories contribute to cultural heritage. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Reference People inherit culture without necessarily realising it. Taking the time to talk to older generations helps them to become aware of this transmission. (Designed as an original youth work activity; adaptable to different national and local contexts.) Overview Recommended: 1.5 hours Extended: 2 hours with real interviews Interviews can be done later. For participants: What did I learn from the interview? Did any stories surprise me? Was it easy or difficult for me to ask open questions? Did the conversation reveal cultural aspects I had never thought about? For youth workers: The facilitator emphasises that listening carefully is a key skill for cultural documentation. Learning objectives Culture is often transmitted through stories shared between generations. Older generations hold valuable memories about traditions, everyday practices and local history. Through interviews, young people can collect stories about: traditions and celebrations, food practices, neighbourhood history, family memories, cultural changes over time. This activity introduces participants to oral history methods in a simple and accessible way. It also strengthens intergenerational dialogue. By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.understand that culture is transmitted through personal experiences and memories 2.learn how to conduct a simple interview, practise asking open-ended questions, develop listening and observation skills 3.collect cultural stories that can later be used in storytelling or media projects. This activity helps participants discover how personal memories contribute to cultural heritage. Introduction to cultural interviews (~10–15 minutes) The facilitator explains that many cultural traditions are transmitted through stories and experiences. Participants are asked to think about people who might have interesting stories about culture, such as: parents or grandparents, neighbours, community members, local artisans or shop owners. The facilitator explains that the goal of the activity is not to test knowledge but to listen and learn from others’ experiences. Preparing interview questions (~20 minutes) Participants work in small groups to prepare open-ended interview questions. The facilitator explains the difference between: Closed questions (questions that can be answered with yes/no) Open questions (questions that encourage storytelling) Examples of open questions include: Who taught you this tradition?, When did you first learn it?, What does this practice mean to you?, How has this tradition changed over time?, Do you have a personal memory connected to it? Participants write their questions and select the ones they would like to use during the interview. Practicing the Interview (~30–40 minutes) Participants practise the interview process in pairs. One participant plays the role of the interviewer and the other plays the role of the interviewee. The interviewer practises: asking open questions, listening attentively, asking follow-up questions, taking notes or recording the conversation After the first round, participants switch roles. This exercise helps participants become more comfortable with the interview method. Conducting real interviews (optional extension) If possible, participants can conduct real interviews with people from their community. Examples of interview themes: traditional food and cooking practices, childhood games and activities, local cultural events, migration stories, neighbourhood transformations Participants can document the interview through: written notes, audio recordings, photos (with permission) Methodology Steps: Collecting cultural memories through interviews 54 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 90 min. "},{"u":"chapter-2-activity-4.html","t":"Activity 4 · Cultural Layers - Understanding Identity Through the “Russian Doll” Model","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":" Material list 1.A personal visual representation of the cultural influences shaping participants’ identities. 2.Greater awareness of the multiple cultural environments to which individuals belong. 3.Identification of cultural topics for further exploration. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Reference People inherit culture without necessarily realising it. Taking the time to talk to older generations helps them to become aware of this transmission. (Designed as an original youth work activity; adaptable to different national and local contexts.) Printed templates of Russian dolls (Matryoshka shapes) in different sizes Paper and coloured pencils or markers Scissors (optional) Overview Minimum: 1 hour Recommended: 1.5 hours For participants: What did I discover about my own cultural influences? Did I realise that I belong to multiple cultural environments? Were there similarities with other participants’ cultural layers? Which cultural topics I would like to explore further? For youth workers: The facilitator highlights that understanding one’s own cultural layers helps people become more open to the experiences of others. Learning objectives This activity uses the metaphor of Russian nesting dolls to help participants explore their cultural identity. Each doll represents a layer of influence that shapes who we are. Just as Russian dolls contain several figures inside one another, people are shaped by different environments and experiences throughout their lives. These layers may include: family traditions and values, school and education, friendships and peer groups, local community influences, digital culture and global influences, personal choices and experiences. Through this exercise, participants discover that cultural identity is complex and dynamic, influenced by both inherited traditions and personal experiences. By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.understand that cultural identity is shaped by multiple influences 2.reflect on the different cultural environments that have influenced their lives 3.recognise that each person belongs to several cultural groups 4.identify cultural themes that could become topics for documentation projects. This activity helps participants understand that identity is not fixed but is built through multiple layers of socialisation and experiences Introduction to cultural layers (~10–15 minutes) Participants are shown how each doll contains another one inside it, representing different layers. The facilitator explains that cultural identity works in a similar way. Examples of cultural layers may include: family background and traditions, language and education, social environment, hobbies and interests, digital communities. Participants are encouraged to reflect on how different influences shape their identity. Creating personal cultural dolls (~20–30 minutes) Each participant receives a set of Russian doll templates representing different layers. Participants fill each layer with elements that influenced their lives. Examples may include: Inner doll – family culture (food traditions, language spoken at home, values) Second layer – school and education (learning experiences, teachers, subjects) Third layer – friends and peer groups (shared interests, music, hobbies) Fourth layer – broader social influences (local community, neighbourhood, cultural events) Outer layer – personal identity (values, choices, personal experiences) Participants can write words, draw symbols or illustrate their influences. Sharing and Reflection (~20–30 minutes) Participants present their cultural dolls to the group or in small groups. They explain: which influences shaped them the most, which cultural elements they inherited, which ones they chose or developed themselves This sharing often reveals both similarities and differences between participants. It also helps participants realise that everyone carries multiple cultural influences. Identifying cultural topics (~10–15 minutes) The facilitator invites participants to reflect on which elements from their dolls could become topics for cultural exploration. Examples might include: family recipes, cultural traditions, local music scenes, neighbourhood history, youth cultures, migration stories. Participants select one or two ideas that could be developed into a documentation project. Methodology Steps: Cultural Layers - Understanding Identity Through the “Russian Doll” Model 55 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 90 min. "},{"u":"chapter-2-activity-5.html","t":"Activity 5 · Exploring culture through people, places and time","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":" Material list 1.Recognising that there are many different sources, as well as different categories of sources. 2.Recognising the importance of choosing sources that are relevant to one’s cultural development. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Reference People inherit culture without necessarily realising it. Taking the time to talk to older generations helps them to become aware of this transmission. (Designed as an original youth work activity; adaptable to different national and local contexts.) Printed cards with different profiles or source examples Pens and paper Overview Recommended: 45 minutes For participants: Which sources seemed most interesting for me to explore? Did I discover new ways of finding cultural information? Which type of source might provide the richest stories? For youth workers: The facilitator emphasises that combining different types of sources often leads to a deeper understanding of culture. Learning objectives Before collecting cultural information, participants need to understand where cultural knowledge can be found. This activity introduces a simple framework to help participants identify potential sources of information. Participants explore three dimensions: People: individuals who hold knowledge or experiences Places: locations where cultural practices take place Time: historical or generational perspectives Using these three dimensions helps participants map possible sources related to their cultural topic. By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.identify different types of cultural sources 2.understand that culture can be explored through people, places and historical context 3.develop strategies for finding information related to their topic. This exercise helps participants understand that cultural information can come from many different sources. Mapping cultural sources (~20 minutes) Participants work in small groups. Each group receives a set of cards representing different possible sources. Examples may include: • local residents • family members • artisans or shop owners • cultural organisations • neighbourhood locations • archives or historical documents Groups classify the cards according to the three dimensions: > people > places > time They then discuss how each source could help them learn more about their topic. Cultural Exploration (~10 minutes) Participants select a few sources from their map and discuss how they could explore them. For example: • interviewing a person • visiting a location • searching for historical information Methodology Steps: Exploring culture through people, places and time 56 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 45 min. "},{"u":"chapter-2-activity-6.html","t":"Activity 6 · Exploring cultural communities online","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":" Material list 1.Reframe digital sources as just one source among many. 2.Maintain a critical perspective on digital sources to enhance research. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Reference People inherit culture without necessarily realising it. Taking the time to talk to older generations helps them to become aware of this transmission. (Designed as an original youth work activity; adaptable to different national and local contexts.) Internet access Smartphones, tablets or computers Paper and pens for note-taking Toolkit: See the digital resources for cultural exploration Overview Recommended: 1.5 hours For participants: What types of cultural information did I find online? Did I discover communities I did not know about before? Was it easy or difficult to identify potential interviewees for me? How can digital platforms help me document culture? For youth workers: The facilitator emphasises that digital platforms are only one type of source, and they should ideally be combined with interviews, observations and other forms of cultural exploration. Learning objectives Today, many cultural communities share information, stories and experiences through digital platforms and social networks. These online spaces can help young people: discover cultural practices, identify communities connected to their topic, find people who could be interviewed, observe how culture is expressed online. This activity introduces participants to the idea that digital environments can be used as research tools for cultural exploration. Participants will explore online platforms to identify cultural information and possible sources related to their chosen topic. By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.identify digital platforms that provide cultural information 2.recognise online communities as potential sources of cultural knowledge 3.identify potential interviewees or informants related to their topic 4.develop curiosity and critical thinking when exploring online sources. This activity helps participants understand that digital platforms can be used not only for communication, but also as tools for cultural exploration and research. Introduction to digital cultural exploration (~10 minutes) The facilitator explains that digital platforms can provide valuable insights into cultural practices. Examples of platforms include: social media groups, community pages, cultural forums, online archives or mapping platforms. Participants are encouraged to think about how people share cultural experiences online. The facilitator introduces the concept of community mapping, which means identifying communities, people and places connected to a specific cultural topic. Exploring digital communities (~30–40 minutes) Participants work in small groups. Each group selects a cultural topic (for example food traditions, local music, neighbourhood culture, migration stories, etc.). They then explore digital platforms to identify: • online communities related to their topic • people who share experiences or knowledge about the topic • events, discussions or posts that reveal cultural practices Participants take notes on what they discover. They should focus on identifying potential sources of cultural information. Identifying potential informants (~20 minutes) Participants now reflect on the people they discovered during their exploration. They identify individuals or groups who could be interesting interview partners. For example: a community organiser, a local artist, a food blogger, someone sharing cultural memories online. Participants discuss why these people could provide valuable insights into the chosen cultural topic. Preparing contact messages (~20 minutes) Participants imagine that they want to contact one of these potential informants. Together with the facilitator, they draft a short message presenting: their project, the cultural topic they are exploring, why they would like to speak with this person. They also prepare a few questions they might ask during an interview. This step helps participants understand how to approach people respectfully and clearly. Methodology Steps: Exploring cultural communities online 57 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 90 min. "},{"u":"chapter-2-activity-7.html","t":"Activity 7 · At the table","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":" Material list 1.Raise awareness of everyday actions and their cultural significance. 2.Discover just how valuable observation techniques are for understanding the meaning of local culture. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Reference People inherit culture without necessarily realising it. Taking the time to talk to older generations helps them to become aware of this transmission. (Designed as an original youth work activity; adaptable to different national and local contexts.) Question cards to guide the discussion Paper and pens for note-taking Smartphone or recording device (optional) Camera or phone for photos or video (optional) Overview Recommended: 3 hours Extended: 4 hours Can be split into two sessions. Partly outside the workshop. For participants: What did you learn about your own culture during this workshop? Did the conversation reveal stories you did not expect? What surprised me the most? What cultural elements were visible through food and cooking? For youth workers: Did participants feel comfortable in the experiences? Did the activities encourage curiosity? Learning objectives Food is an important part of cultural identity. Recipes, cooking techniques and shared meals often carry stories about family traditions, migration, celebrations and everyday life. In this activity, participants explore culture through a dish, a recipe or a shared meal. By observing gestures, objects and conversations around food, participants learn how cultural practices are transmitted informally between generations. This activity combines several research methods: observation, informal interviews, documentation through notes, photos or audio. By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.understand how everyday practices such as cooking can reflect cultural traditions 2.collect cultural information through observation and conversation 3.practise asking open-ended questions during an informal interview 4.develop listening and observation skills 5.recognise the importance of everyday objects and gestures in cultural transmission. This activity helps participants discover how food and shared meals can become entry points for exploring culture and collecting stories. Preparing the activity (~20 minutes) The facilitator introduces the activity and explains that participants will explore culture through food. Participants discuss possible examples: a traditional family recipe, a dish connected to celebrations, a recipe linked to migration or cultural exchange The facilitator introduces the idea of open-ended questions and explains that curiosity and listening are key tools for cultural exploration. Preparing Questions (~20 minutes) Participants work in small groups and prepare questions that they could ask during a conversation about the dish. Examples of open-ended questions include: Who taught you how to prepare this dish?, On what occasions do you cook this?, With whom do you usually share this meal?, Do you have a memory connected to this dish?, What makes this dish successful or special? Participants may also prepare observation questions such as: What tools or objects are used?, What gestures are important during the preparation? Conducting the cultural exploration (~40–60 minutes) Participants observe or recreate the preparation of a dish. They can: interview the person preparing the dish, observe cooking gestures and techniques, note the ingredients and objects used, record sounds, photos or videos (with permission). Participants are encouraged to pay attention not only to what is said, but also to what is done. Gestures, tools and shared moments often reveal important cultural meanings. Documenting cultural information (~15 minutes) After the activity, participants write down what they learned. They can document: the story of the dish, cultural meanings attached to it, memories shared during the conversation, observations about gestures and objects. Participants may also reflect on how the dish relates to identity, family traditions or community practices. Methodology Steps: At the table 58 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - hrs "},{"u":"chapter-2-activity-8.html","t":"Activity 8 · Interview circle: Collecting cultural heritage stories","c":"Chapter 2 · Local Cultural Documentation","x":" Material list Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Interview circle: Collecting cultural heritage stories 4 hrs Participants will be able to storyboard and map, in a similar way to Chapter 3, but the output this time differs, as the result of the interview is to create potential podcast content that serves as raw material for future podcast or blog narratives. Reference Storytelling for Youth Work: Multimedia Guide Recording devices (mobile phones or simple audio recorders) Printouts of interview prompt sheets Pens, sticky notes for mapping content Toolkit: Interview questions sheet + consent form Overview Recommended: 4 hours Can be split into two sessions. Warm-up – 30 min Interview briefing – 30 min Interviews – 90 min Sharing – 60 min Mapping – 30 min For participants: What techniques helped me get a story from my partner? Which stories felt strongest - and how might they be shared? What challenges did I encounter when listening or asking follow-ups? How could I develop these interviews into podcast or blog content afterward? Learning objectives An interactive workshop where participants learn the craft of interview storytelling focused on cultural memories or heritage narratives. The activity emphasises interpersonal skills and narrative themes relevant to real lived experience. By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.practise interview techniques suited to podcast content creation 2.strengthen listening and conversational skills 3.identify compelling stories within group narratives 4.recognise and collect strong narrative moments that can be reused in future podcast or blog content. Warm-up & story prompting (~30 min): The facilitator introduces key interview questions and listening roles, and he/she prepares a brief icebreaker where participants share a personal memory related to heritage. Interview skill briefing (~30 min): There will be a showcase with one volunteer pair, using simple frameworks: open questions, follow-ups, non-leading prompts. Paired Interviews (~90 min): Participants take turns interviewing one another (10–15 min each). Focus topic: a remembered cultural story, family tradition, local history moment. Share & group reflection (~60 min): Each pair plays back (or reads narrative notes) of a favourite moment, and the group helps identify what made a segment compelling. Identifying usable content (~30 min): Participants review their interviews to select key quotes, moments, or themes that could be reused later in a podcast or blog (without structuring a full narrative yet). Interview cards to download: Methodology Steps: 59 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - Itinerary of an object Itinerary of a tradition Itinerary of a monument "},{"u":"chapter-3.html#infographic-summary","t":"I N F O G R A P H I C S U M M A R Y","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Visual summary of this chapter — click to enlarge or download. Download infographic (PDF)"},{"u":"chapter-3.html#intro","t":"Introduction","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"In Chapter 2, young people explored culture by observing their environment, interviewing people, and collecting stories, memories and local knowledge. The next step is to transform this material into meaningful stories . This chapter introduces cultural storytelling as a way of organising and sharing cultural experiences. Storytelling helps young people move from simply collecting information to creating narratives that others can understand, connect with and reflect on. In youth work contexts, storytelling allows participants to give meaning to what they have discovered . By selecting important elements, choosing a point of view and structuring a narrative, young people learn how to make cultural heritage visible and accessible . At this stage, participants can already consider their intended audience and a possible format, as these choices may influence how the story is structured. This chapter focuses on the creative and narrative process , not on technical production. It helps youth workers guide participants in answering key questions such as: What story do we want to tell? Why does it matter? For whom are we telling it? What is the best angle to approach it? Through practical methods and workshops, participants learn how to transform collected cultural material into stories that can later be adapted into different media formats such as podcasts, blogs, short videos or digital exhibitions. In this way, storytelling becomes the bridge between cultural exploration (Chapter 2) and media production (Chapter 4)."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#digcomp-3-0-connection-sintro","t":"DigComp 3.0 connection","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"This chapter mainly supports Digital content creation, helping young people select, structure and prepare cultural material for meaningful communication."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#learning-objectives-sintro","t":"Learning objectives","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"By the end of this chapter, youth workers will be able to: understand storytelling as a way of organising and interpreting cultural experiences help young people transform collected cultural material into clear narratives support participants in identifying a strong angle, message and audience introduce simple storytelling methods adapted to non-formal education prepare participants to adapt their stories into digital formats in the next chapter. Sections Section 1 | The power of storytelling Section 2 | Building a cultural narrative Section 3 | The key principles of effective cultural storytelling Section 4 | Practical workshop & case studies"},{"u":"chapter-3.html#section-1-storytelling-as-cultural-translation","t":"Section 1 · Storytelling as cultural translation","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":""},{"u":"chapter-3.html#storytelling-in-youth-empowerment-s1","t":"Storytelling in youth empowerment","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":""},{"u":"chapter-3.html#why-storytelling-matters-in-youth-work-s1","t":"Why storytelling matters in youth work","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Stories are one of the oldest ways humans share knowledge , experiences and values. Through stories, people explain the past, describe the present and imagine the future. Storytelling also involves making choices and interpreting reality . Youth workers should encourage participants to reflect on their point of view , what they choose to show, and what remains invisible . In youth work, storytelling plays an important role because it allows young people to express their own perspectives and experiences. It also helps them connect personal stories with collective cultural heritage . When young people learn to tell stories, they develop important skills such as: expressing ideas and emotions listening to different perspectives interpreting cultural experiences communicating with different audiences building confidence in their own voice Storytelling is therefore not only a communication tool. It is also a process of reflection and empowerment ."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#storytelling-as-cultural-translation-s1","t":"Storytelling as cultural translation","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"When young people tell a cultural story, they do more than simply repeat information. They select what is meaningful and present it in a way that others can understand. In this sense, storytelling can be seen as a form of cultural translation. Participants interpret cultural practices, memories and experiences and translate them into narratives that make sense for others. This is especially important when working with topics such as: local traditions family memories neighbourhood histories community experiences everyday cultural practices Through storytelling, culture becomes something that can be shared, discussed and understood beyond the original context."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#from-cultural-material-to-narrative-s1","t":"From cultural material to narrative","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"In Chapter 2, participants collected cultural material through observation, interviews and exploration. This material may include: personal stories memories shared by community members photographs, sounds or objects observations of everyday cultural practices information found in archives or online sources However, collected material does not automatically become a story . Storytelling begins when participants start to: choose what is important organise the information identify a clear focus give meaning to what they discovered This process transforms cultural information into a narrative that others can follow and connect with. Key message Cultural differences are a source of richness . Let us learn to explain and share them through the art of storytelling."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#section-2-building-a-cultural-narrative","t":"Section 2 · Building a cultural narrative","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Key message Although collecting cultural information is an important first step, storytelling only begins when participants decide how to organise and present what they discovered in Chapter 2’s workshops. Young people often gather a large amount of information during interviews, observations or research. However, trying to include everything usually makes the story confusing or difficult to follow. Good storytelling requires selection, focus and structure . Youth workers can support participants by guiding them through a few simple steps that help transform cultural material into a clear and engaging narrative ."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#narrative-building-s2","t":"Narrative building","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":""},{"u":"chapter-3.html#1-choosing-a-clear-angle-s2","t":"1. Choosing a clear angle","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"One of the most common challenges in storytelling is that participants try to talk about too many things at once. Instead, a strong cultural story usually focuses on one main idea or perspective. This is called the angle of the story . The angle helps answer questions such as: What is the most interesting aspect of this story? What do we want the audience to understand? What makes this story meaningful or unique? For example: Instead of telling a general story about a neighbourhood, participants might focus on: how one street has changed over time the story of a local shop that everyone knows a place where young people usually meet a tradition that still exists today By narrowing the focus , the story becomes more concrete and more engaging."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#2-identifying-audience-and-purpose-s2","t":"2. Identifying audience and purpose","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Before telling a story, it is useful to think about who the story is for. A story told for local residents may be different from a story created for young people from another country. Participants can reflect on questions such as: Who will listen to or read this story? What do we want them to understand or feel? What might be new or surprising for them? Which format could best serve this story and its audience? For example: podcast, blog, short video, social media story or exhibition. Clarifying the audience helps participants choose what information to include and how to present it. For example, when sharing local cultural stories with an international audience, it may be necessary to explain certain traditions or references that local people already know."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#3-structuring-the-story-s2","t":"3. Structuring the story","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Even a simple story benefits from a basic structure . Participants do not need complex storytelling theories. A simple narrative structure is often enough. A cultural story can be organised in four steps : Beginning – Context Introduce the place, person or situation. Help the audience understand where the story takes place. Focus – What is this story about? Present the central element of the story: a person, a place, an object or a memory. Development – What happens or what is discovered? Share observations, memories or experiences that reveal something interesting about the culture being explored. Meaning – Why does this story matter? Explain what this story tells us about the community, the culture or the experience being shared. This structure helps participants organise their ideas and avoid losing the audience"},{"u":"chapter-3.html#4-revealing-the-visible-and-the-invisible-s2","t":"4. Revealing the visible and the invisible","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"One of the strengths of cultural storytelling is the ability to move from what we can easily see to what is less visible but equally important. Many cultural stories start with something tangible , such as: a place a traditional dish an object a local celebration a daily routine However, behind these visible elements there are often deeper meanings : memories values identities changes over time hidden stories within the community Encouraging participants to explore both the visible and invisible aspects of culture helps create richer and more meaningful stories."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#5-preparing-the-story-for-different-formats-s2","t":"5. Preparing the story for different formats","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Participants do not need to make a final decision about the format at this stage, but considering a likely format can help them shape the story for its intended audience . Some stories are particularly effective when told through voice and sound , especially when interviews, memories or conversations are central. Other stories may benefit from images or movement, for example when places, gestures or visual details play an important role. Some stories may also work well as written narratives , especially when reflection and explanation are important. The purpose of this reflection is not to make a final decision yet, but to help participants understand that different stories may require different forms of expression . The next chapter will explore how these stories can be developed into concrete media formats such as podcasts and blogs."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#section-3-the-key-principles-of-effective-cultural-storytell","t":"Section 3 · The key principles of effective cultural storytelling","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Storytelling is not only about creativity. It is also about organising ideas and making choices. This section is theoretical and the following section translates these concepts into practical workshop activities. For young participants, the challenge is often not the lack of stories, but the difficulty of deciding what to include and how to present it. Youth workers can support this process by introducing simple storytelling methods that help participants move from raw material to a coherent narrative . The following approaches are easy to apply in youth work contexts and do not require technical expertise."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#1-start-from-real-experiences-s3","t":"1. Start from real experiences","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Cultural storytelling becomes meaningful when it is rooted in real experiences and observations. Participants should be encouraged to build their stories from: interviews conducted with community members observations made during local explorations personal memories or family traditions everyday cultural practices objects, places or sounds that carry meaning Starting from real experiences helps ensure that stories remain authentic and relatable. Youth workers can remind participants that storytelling does not require extraordinary events. Very often, meaningful cultural stories emerge from ordinary situations."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#2-cultural-storytelling-should-be-ethical-s3","t":"2. Cultural storytelling should be ethical","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Ethical storytelling involves telling stories in a conscious and responsible manner. It ensures that individuals and groups are portrayed respectfully, without reducing their complexity to clichés or stereotypes . It incorporates the concept of consent , ensuring that the stories shared-particularly those based on real-life experiences-are shared with the agreement of those concerned. This approach invites us to question our perspective and how our stories influence others’ perceptions. It encourages young people to tell stories in order to connect, understand and celebrate the diversity of experiences , rather than to exclude or judge."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#3-focus-on-concrete-details-s3","t":"3. Focus on concrete details","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Stories become engaging when they include specific details. Instead of speaking in general terms, participants should be encouraged to describe: a specific place a particular moment a gesture or action an object or symbol a personal memory For example, instead of saying: «People in our town celebrate many traditions.» Participants could describe: «Every year, on the first Sunday of spring, families gather in the town square to share homemade dishes.» Concrete details help the audience visualise the story and connect emotionally with it."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#4-select-what-matters-most-s3","t":"4. Select what matters most","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"When participants collect information, they often accumulate more material than they can use. This approach also supports responsible digital practices by limiting unnecessary recording and focusing on meaningful content. A key step in storytelling is therefore selection . Youth workers can help participants identify: the most interesting moment the most meaningful quote from an interview the element that best represents the cultural story A useful question to ask is: «If we could keep only three elements from everything we collected, what would they be?» This helps participants focus on the essential parts of the story ."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#5-organise-the-story-visually-with-a-storyboard-s3","t":"5. Organise the story visually with a storyboard","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"A storyboard is a simple tool that helps participants organise their story step by step. Instead of writing the entire story immediately, participants create a visual sequence of the main elements. A basic storyboard may include: The beginning – introducing the context or place The main focus – presenting the central person, object or situation The key moment – the most interesting discovery or memory The meaning – what this story tells us about the culture or community Storyboards help participants: structure their ideas identify missing elements organise the flow of the narrative They also make it easier to later adapt the story into different formats such as podcasts or blogs."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#6-connecting-personal-stories-to-broader-culture-s3","t":"6. Connecting personal stories to broader culture","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"A strong cultural story often connects individual experiences to collective meaning. Participants can reflect on questions such as: What does this story reveal about our community? Does this story represent a broader tradition or cultural practice? How does this story connect past and present? Encouraging participants to move from personal experience to cultural meaning helps create stories that are both personal and relevant for others ."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#section-4-practical-workshops-case-studies","t":"Section 4 · Practical workshops & case studies","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"The following workshops help participants move from collected cultural material to structured storytelling. The activities are designed for non-formal education contexts and can be adapted depending on group size, available time and participants’ experience. The workshops follow a progressive approach: 1. Understanding what makes a story engaging 2. Choosing a clear angle 3. Structuring the story 4. Producing a short cultural story Youth workers may choose to organise these activities during a single workshop or across several sessions. Storytelling is the foundation for digital storytelling formats such as podcasts, blogs or video narratives, which will be explored in the next chapter. 1 What makes a story engaging? View activity PDF 2 From collected material to story angle View activity PDF 3 Storyboard workshop View activity PDF"},{"u":"chapter-3.html#case-study-s4-1","t":"4.1. Case study 1 – Inspiring method","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Source: Interview with Séverine Enjolras (workshop co-facilitator) Resource: https://www.acap-cinema.com/rembobinez-le-film-en-questions/"},{"u":"chapter-3.html#rembobinez-exploring-local-memory-through-images-and-questio-s4","t":"Rembobinez: Exploring local memory through images and questions","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":""},{"u":"chapter-3.html#context-s4","t":"Context","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"The Rembobinez project was designed to help participants analyse a documentary film . The work with young people was based on a deck of cards, each presenting a question to analyse a different aspect of the film. The facilitators and the French teacher encouraged the young people to reflect on the notion of perspective, how the film looks at its subject, while also exploring emotions and personal responses. The workshops aimed both to provide media and image education and to create a space where young people’s voices were valued. They also served as a space for developing critical thinking ."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#how-the-method-works-s4","t":"How the method works","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"Participants work with a set of cards containing questions related to a film or an audiovisual archive. The questions invite participants to explore different aspects of storytelling , such as: personal memories connected to an image or place emotions or impressions triggered by a scene links between past and present different interpretations of the same cultural material The cards act as conversation starters , encouraging dialogue and collective storytelling. Through discussion, participants begin to identify meaningful narratives that can later be developed into cultural stories."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#why-this-method-works-well-with-young-people-s4","t":"Why this method works well with young people","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"This approach encourages active participation and multiple perspectives . Instead of giving a single interpretation, the activity invites young people to: express their own ideas and experiences listen to different viewpoints explore cultural heritage through discussion develop narrative thinking By engaging with images and questions, participants gradually move from observation to interpretation and storytelling ."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#what-youth-workers-can-take-from-this-example-s4","t":"What youth workers can take from this example","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"This case study shows that storytelling can start with simple facilitation tools . Youth workers can use similar approaches to help participants explore cultural material, for example by using: discussion cards photographs or archive images objects connected to local culture personal memories shared by community members These tools make it easier for participants to identify meaningful stories before producing media content."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#link-with-this-chapter-s4","t":"Link with this chapter","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"This method illustrates an important step in cultural storytelling: interpreting and discussing cultural material before turning it into a narrative . It helps participants identify stories that can later be developed into podcasts, blogs or other digital storytelling formats explored in the following chapters."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#case-study-s4-2","t":"4.2. Case study 2 – From objects to stories: A participatory storytelling method","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"This approach is inspired by participatory cultural practices developed in France, including those implemented by the National Museum of Immigration History, as well as popular education organisations such as Les Petits Débrouillards and CEMEA, which use objects, personal narratives and collective expression as tools for learning and storytelling. Les Petits Débrouillards – educational catalogue (PDF) www.lespetitsdebrouillardsgrandest.org/IMG/pdf/catalogues_pedagogique_ge.pdf ↗ CEMEA – Culture and artistic practices cemea.asso.fr/les-champs-d-action/culture-et-pratiques-artistiques ↗ National Museum of Immigration History – Resources www.histoire-immigration.fr"},{"u":"chapter-3.html#context-s4-2","t":"Context","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"This case study is inspired by participatory cultural practices developed in France, including approaches used in institutions such as the National Museum of Immigration History, where personal objects are used to tell migration stories. Building on this approach, youth participants are invited to create symbolic objects or “totems” representing aspects of their cultural identity, family history or lived experiences. Each object becomes a support for storytelling, allowing participants to express their story in a tangible and visual way . Learning objectives: Understand how objects can carry cultural and personal meaning Develop storytelling skills through symbolic representation Reflect on identity, memory and cultural heritage Build confidence in sharing personal or collective stories Connect individual experiences with broader social and cultural narratives Activities and results: Participants first explore examples of objects used in museums to tell personal or migration stories. They then: reflect on their own cultural background, memories or experiences select or create objects representing key aspects of their story assemble these elements into a personal or collective totem present and explain their object to the group The result is a series of visual and narrative artefacts , each representing a unique cultural story. Content and methodological approach: The approach combines cultural exploration, symbolic thinking and storytelling . Participants move from: reflection (What is part of my story?) selection (Which elements represent it?) representation (How can I show it through an object?) narration (How do I explain its meaning?) This method allows storytelling to emerge through both verbal and non-verbal expression , making it accessible to diverse groups. Target group: Young people (15–30 years old) Youth groups in non-formal education settings Participants from diverse cultural or migration backgrounds Key elements of digital and media culture: Understanding how stories can be communicated through different formats (not only text or speech) Developing narrative skills before digital production Reflecting on how cultural stories are represented and shared Preparing content that can later be adapted into digital formats (podcasts, blogs, short videos) Impact: Increased confidence in expressing personal and cultural identity Stronger connection between individual stories and collective heritage Development of storytelling and communication skills Enhanced group cohesion through shared narratives Greater awareness of cultural diversity and multiple perspectives Why this is important for Ourculture: This approach reflects a key principle of the OURCULTURE project: starting from lived experiences to build meaningful cultural narratives. By using objects as storytelling tools, participants learn that stories do not only exist in digital formats, but can first be thought, shaped and embodied . This method also prepares participants for the next steps of the guide: structuring narratives (Chapter 3) producing media (Chapter 4) sharing stories responsibly (Chapter 5) It reinforces the idea that digital storytelling begins with intention, meaning and reflection, not with technology ."},{"u":"chapter-3.html#from-storytelling-to-media-creation-s4","t":"From storytelling to media creation","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":"In this chapter, participants learned how to transform cultural material into meaningful narratives. Through storytelling methods and practical workshops, young people explored how to: identify the most important elements of a cultural story choose a clear narrative angle organise information into a structured story prepare a narrative before producing media content Not all stories need to become complex productions. Simple, well-prepared stories are often the most impactful . This process is essential because strong digital storytelling begins with clear ideas and thoughtful preparation . Once participants have developed a clear story, they can begin to think about how to share it with others . The next chapter explores how these stories can be transformed into digital media formats , such as podcasts and blogs. Participants will learn how to produce and share their stories while continuing to apply the principles introduced in this guide: meaningful storytelling, accessibility and responsible use of digital tools . Through these practices, young people can use digital media not only to communicate, but also to preserve cultural heritage, express their perspectives and contribute to their communities. ← Back Ch. 2 · Local Cultural Documentation Next → Ch. 4 · Podcasting and blogging"},{"u":"chapter-3-activity-1.html","t":"Activity 1 · What makes a story engaging?","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":" Material list 1.The ideas are better organised. 2.A better impact on stories and the ability to share information about culture. 3.Increased self-confidence. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Short example texts or stories Paper and pens Flipchart or board Overview Recommended: 50 minutes Extended: 60 minutes For participants: What did I learn about telling stories? How can details and context improve my story? How can these storytelling techniques help me when sharing cultural stories? Can I figure out how to use what I’ve learned to tell cultural stories? For youth workers: Does the activity encourage participants to share stories about their culture? Learning objectives This activity helps participants understand the difference between simply presenting information and telling a story. Participants compare two versions of the same information: one presented in a neutral or factual way, and another organised as a narrative with details and a clear focus. Through discussion and a short rewriting exercise, participants discover how storytelling techniques make cultural information more engaging and meaningful. By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.distinguish between raw information and engaging storytelling 2.identify elements that make a story interesting and meaningful 3.understand how details and narrative structure strengthen a story 4.transform simple information into a short narrative. Reading or listening to the two versions (~10 minutes) The facilitator presents two short texts or stories based on the same topic. One version presents information in a factual way, while the second version includes narrative elements such as concrete details, context and a clear focus. Group analysis (~20 minutes) Participants discuss the differences between the two versions. The facilitator asks guiding questions such as: Which version was more interesting? What made the difference between the two stories? Which elements helped create a stronger narrative? Key elements identified by participants are written on a board. Short rewriting exercise (~20 minutes) Participants work in small groups. Each group receives a short piece of information (for example about a place, a tradition or an object). Their task is to transform the information into a short narrative by adding details and structure. Methodology Steps: What makes a story engaging? 79 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 50 min. "},{"u":"chapter-3-activity-2.html","t":"Activity 2 · From collected material to story angle","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":" Material list 1.Be aware of the wealth of a culture. 2.Identify the criteria for selecting material to tell a cultural story. 3.Share a reflection. 4.The ability to make a choice. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection notes and materials collected during previous activities sticky notes large sheet of paper or board Overview Recommended: 60 minutes For participants: What did I learn with this exercise in laying everything out? Was it difficult to choose only one angle? How can narrowing the focus make a story stronger? For youth workers: The facilitator highlights that good storytelling often comes from focusing on one clear idea rather than trying to tell everything at once. Learning objectives After collecting information through interviews, observation or research, participants often have a large amount of material. This activity helps participants identify the most meaningful elements and select one clear focus for their story. By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.review and select relevant cultural material collected during previous activities 2.identify possible themes or story ideas 3.choose a clear narrative angle 4.explain why a selected story matters. Reviewing collected material (~15 minutes) Participants review the notes, quotes, observations or images they collected earlier. They write key ideas, quotes or themes on sticky notes. Identifying possible story ideas (~20 minutes) Participants group the sticky notes into possible themes or story ideas. These may include personal memories, local traditions, stories about places, or experiences shared during interviews. Choosing the story angle (~25 minutes) Participants select one idea and answer three questions: What is this story about? Why is this story interesting or meaningful? What do we want others to understand from this story? This helps participants identify the central angle of their narrative. Methodology Steps: From collected material to story angle 80 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 60 min. "},{"u":"chapter-3-activity-3.html","t":"Activity 3 · Storyboard workshop","c":"Chapter 3 · Cultural Storytelling","x":" Material list 1.A clear understanding of what constitutes a good story. 2.Develop a more critical view of the content they consume. 3.Improve their ability to share information about culture. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection storyboard templates or blank paper divided into four sections pens or markers Overview Recommended: 60 minutes Extended: 75 minutes For participants: Did the storyboard help me clarify the story? What changes did I make while organising the sequence? Which part of my story seems the most important? For youth workers: The facilitator emphasises that storyboards help organise ideas before recording or writing the story. Learning objectives A storyboard is a simple tool that helps participants organise the sequence of a story before producing it in any media format. This activity allows participants to visualise how their story will unfold and to identify the most important moments of the narrative. By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.organise a story into a clear sequence 2.identify the key moments of a narrative 3.use a storyboard to structure cultural material 4.prepare a story for future media production. Introducing the storyboard method (~10 minutes) The facilitator explains how a storyboard helps organise a story by dividing it into key moments. See section 3 of this chapter: “The key principles of effective cultural storytelling” Choose a likely format Before creating the storyboard, choose a likely format for the story: podcast, blog, short video, social media story or exhibition. The format can still change later. Creating the storyboard (~40 minutes) Participants divide their page into four sections and organise their story as follows: 1. Context – Where does the story take place? 2. Focus – Who or what is at the centre of the story? 3. Key moment – What discovery, event or memory reveals something important? 4. Meaning – What does this story tell us about the culture or community? Participants can draw, write keywords or include quotes. Sharing storyboards (~15–20 minutes) Participants present their storyboard to the group and explain their narrative choices. Methodology Steps: Storyboard workshop 81 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 60 min. "},{"u":"chapter-4.html#infographic-summary","t":"I N F O G R A P H I C S U M M A R Y","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Visual summary of this chapter — click to enlarge or download. Download infographic (PDF)"},{"u":"chapter-4.html#intro","t":"Introduction","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"This chapter introduces podcasting and blogging as digital storytelling tools that youth workers can use to engage young people with cultural heritage, civic participation and creative expression. Through accessible technologies and participatory methods, young participants learn how to capture stories , document cultural experiences and share narratives with wider audiences . Building on the storytelling work developed in Chapter 3, this chapter moves from structured cultural narratives to concrete media production and responsible sharing , using podcasts and blogs as accessible tools to bring those stories to life."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#digcomp-3-0-connection-sintro","t":"DigComp 3.0 connection","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"This chapter mainly supports Digital content creation and Communication and collaboration, helping young people create, adapt and share digital cultural content responsibly."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#learning-objectives-sintro","t":"Learning objectives","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"By the end of this chapter, youth workers will be able to: understand the role of podcasts and blogs in youth civic participation and cultural storytelling introduce basic technical foundations of podcasting and blogging facilitate creative workshops where young people produce audio or written cultural stories support youth in documenting cultural heritage through interviews, sound recording and storytelling connect digital youth work with cultural heritage engagement and community dialogue. Sections Section 1 | Digital expression & youth voice Section 2 | Technical foundations Section 3 | Practical tools & activities Section 4 | Case studies & best practices"},{"u":"chapter-4.html#section-1-digital-expression-youth-voice","t":"Section 1 · Digital expression & youth voice","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":""},{"u":"chapter-4.html#digital-expression-youth-voice-s1","t":"Digital expression & youth voice","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":""},{"u":"chapter-4.html#why-use-podcasts-and-blogs-in-youth-work-s1","t":"Why use podcasts and blogs in youth work?","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Because they give young people a simple way to share stories, ideas and experiences. Podcasts and blogs offer practical ways for young people to produce and share stories using accessible digital tools. Unlike earlier stages of storytelling (see Chapter 3), these formats focus on recording voices, structuring content for publication, and reaching audiences. Podcasts are particularly engaging because they are based on conversation and storytelling. Young people can interview others, document local places, or discuss issues that matter to them. Across Europe, several youth projects have used podcasting to strengthen digital skills while encouraging participation in community life and democratic dialogue . Blogs complement podcasts by offering a space for written reflection and multimedia storytelling. Through blog posts, young people can document cultural traditions, share personal perspectives, and preserve community memories. Creating podcasts or blogs also helps develop important skills. Participants learn how to organise information, shape narratives, listen carefully to others and communicate responsibly in public spaces. For youth workers, these tools offer a practical way to combine digital youth work, storytelling and cultural heritage exploration, while encouraging young people to become active voices in their communities ."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#relevance-for-ourculture-s1","t":"Relevance for Ourculture","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"For the Ourculture project, this information underscores the power of podcasts and blogs as tools for cultural heritage engagement and youth participation. By facilitating hands-on podcast and blogging activities, the project does more than teach technical skills; it fosters a generation of young storytellers who can capture, interpret, and share cultural narratives. It enhances the quality and innovation of youth work by integrating digital platforms into creative, participatory learning, and it reinforces the role of digital youth work in enabling young people to become not just consumers of media, but producers of culture and civic dialogue ."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#section-2-technical-foundations","t":"Section 2 · Technical foundations","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"The technical elements presented here are not meant for mastery , but to support simple, accessible media production, building on stories already developed (see Chapter 3). To effectively facilitate podcasting and blogging workshops for young people, it is essential to establish a solid set of technical foundations that are accessible, adaptable , and aligned with the goals of digital youth work. While creativity and expression remain central, practical technical skills help participants turn ideas into concrete, shareable content ."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#from-story-to-format-preparing-for-production-s2","t":"2.1. From story to format: Preparing for production","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Before using tools, participants benefit from a simple conceptual foundation : Podcast formats: interview, storytelling, discussion, documentary Blog formats: personal stories, reflections, multimedia posts, themed series Audience awareness: who is this for? What do you want them to feel or learn? Planning basics: write a few key points before recording or writing This step helps participants translate existing story ideas into formats ready for recording or publication."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#recording-basics-and-equipment-s2","t":"2.2. Recording basics and equipment","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Good audio quality does not require expensive equipment -simple tools and mindful use are enough. This is also an opportunity to introduce sustainable and responsible practices . Use what you already have: smartphones, basic headphones with a microphone, or shared devices Avoid unnecessary purchases: prioritise reuse, borrowing, or low-cost equipment Choose a quiet space: reduce background noise instead of relying on editing later Speak clearly and steadily: stay at a consistent distance (about a hand span from the mic) Keep the microphone stable: avoid handling noise or movement during recording Test before recording: record a short sample and listen back Use compressed formats when possible: smaller files are easier to store and share Backup wisely: keep essential files, but avoid unnecessary copies Share responsibly: upload only final or useful versions Recording environment is essential to how your content is perceived: good sound quality is often more important than image quality , as audiences are less tolerant of poor audio. Always prioritise quiet spaces, reduce background noise (traffic, voices, wind), and avoid echo when possible, as these are difficult to fix later. Microphone positioning also plays a key role: keep the microphone at a consistent, close distance (about a hand span), speak clearly and at a steady pace, and avoid sudden movements, touching the mic, or speaking too loudly or too softly. Familiarity with tools empowers participants to experiment confidently. For youth workshops, coordinators can prioritise simplicity: using tools participants already own (like phones) ensures low entry barriers and inclusivity . Producing with intention is a key principle of this chapter. Participants are encouraged to record only what is meaningful, avoid unnecessary data production, and focus on quality rather than quantity . This approach reflects the principle introduced in Chapter 3 - “Record Less, Tell Better” - and supports more sustainable and thoughtful media practices."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#software-and-platforms-s2","t":"2.3. Software and platforms","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"To transform raw recordings into shareable content, participants need access to basic software . Audio editing tools: free solutions like Audacity, GarageBand, or web-based editors for trimming, cleaning, and combining clips. Blogging platforms: WordPress, Blogger, Medium, or similar CMS that are free and user-friendly. Hosting and distribution: understanding how podcasts are published (RSS feeds, anchor sites) and how blogs are updated and shared. Introducing these platforms with a simple demo and guided exploration helps reduce technology anxiety and strengthens digital agency. It is also valuable to connect participants with local resources that already support media creation. Youth centres, community radios, school media labs, or local associations often provide access to equipment, recording spaces, or publishing platforms. Collaborating with these structures (like youth centre web radios or local digital hubs) allows young people to experiment in real contexts , receive guidance from practitioners, and see how their content can reach real audiences. For youth workers, building partnerships with these local actors strengthens support networks and makes projects more sustainable and impactful."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#storytelling-and-narrative-structure-tools-s2","t":"2.4. Storytelling and narrative structure tools","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Technical skills should support storytelling (see Chapter 3 for a deeper exploration of narrative structures and creative approaches). In this section, the focus is on how to technically organise and edit content so that the story is clear and engaging. Storyboards (audio or visual): simple step-by-step planning of scenes or sequences (intro, main content, ending) Interview and script guides: short prompts to structure recordings and avoid missing key points For good interview practices, there’s a practical checklist to follow for each phase: Prepare 4–6 open-ended questions (avoid yes/no questions) Start with easy, comfortable questions before deeper topics Be polite and create a safe, respectful atmosphere Listen actively : stay silent and let the person finish speaking Do not interrupt; allow pauses (they often lead to richer answers) Follow up: ask “why?”, “how?”, “can you give an example?” Keep eye contact and show interest (nod, react naturally) Record in a quiet space and check sound before starting Thank the person and close the interview clearly Scripting exercises can guide participants in structuring content logically and engagingly. For blogs, integrating images, maps, quotations, and hyperlinks fosters multimodal literacy ."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#collaboration-and-workflow-s2","t":"2.5. Collaboration and workflow","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Podcasting and blogging are typically collaborative activities that require coordination among participants. Establishing a clear workflow helps ensure efficient teamwork and smooth content production. Using a shared online folder for project materials-such as audio recordings, written texts, and images- facilitates collective access and organisation. Clear file naming conventions help participants easily identify and locate materials. To avoid version conflicts, it is advisable that only one person edits a file at a time while others review the most recent version. Maintaining version history rather than deleting earlier drafts allows teams to track changes and preserve previous work. Regular sharing of drafts within the group also supports collaborative reflection and constructive feedback throughout the production process."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#ethical-accessibility-considerations-s2","t":"2.6. Ethical & accessibility considerations","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Content creation in podcasts and blogs involves responsibility toward the people, stories, and communities represented. Ethical considerations should therefore guide the entire production process. Permission should be obtained before recording or publishing interviews, voices, or personal stories, and participants should be informed about where and how the content may be shared. The use of respectful and inclusive language is essential to ensure that narratives represent individuals and communities responsibly. European references to ask for permission before publishing participants’ materials: The General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) sets rules on collecting, storing, and sharing personal data National youth work and education guidelines often complement GDPR with specific rules for minors Basic principle: collect only what is necessary , store it securely, and use it transparently Providing a simple consent form template adapted to your activity is strongly recommended, helping youth workers ensure both ethical and legal compliance while protecting participants. When incorporating music, images, or other media, preference should be given to free or open-licensed materials , with appropriate attribution to the original creators. Accessibility is also an important aspect of responsible media production. Clear audio recording, understandable speech, and well-structured text contribute to making content accessible to a broader audience."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#how-this-matters-for-ourculture-s2","t":"How this matters for Ourculture","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"For the Ourculture project, focused on cultural heritage, quality in youth work, innovation, and digital youth work, these technical foundations are crucial. They ensure that participants are not only creative but capable of realising and sharing narratives that matter. The combination of accessible tools, structured workflows , and narrative scaffolding empowers young people to contribute cultural heritage content that resonates locally and beyond. By demystifying technology and situating it within a creative and civic purpose, the project situates youth digital expression at the intersection of heritage preservation, democratic participation , and community storytelling, aligning with Erasmus+ values and producing outputs that can continue to evolve after the pilot activities conclude. At the same time, producing podcasts and blogs involves public communication and representation. These activities invite young people to reflect on responsibility, ethical storytelling , and respectful dialogue in digital spaces. They should also be encouraged to consider the power of images and media in shaping perceptions, and the importance of representing people and situations accurately. Grounding content in real experiences, fieldwork, and authentic voices helps ensure that stories remain meaningful, credible, and respectful. These dimensions connect directly with the principles of digital citizenship, which are explored further in Chapter 5. Photo, video & sound consent form Download the consent form (PDF)"},{"u":"chapter-4.html#section-3-practical-tools-activities","t":"Section 3 · Practical tools & Activities","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":""},{"u":"chapter-4.html#collecting-information-about-culture-s3","t":"Collecting information about culture","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"These activities are designed for young people and can be adapted to different European youth-work contexts. They support the use of podcasting and blogging as tools for cultural heritage storytelling, civic participation and creative expression. It builds on the exploration methods introduced in Chapter 2 (observation, interviews, fieldwork). Here, these approaches are used with a new objective: capturing and transforming material into podcast and blog content. 1 Heritage sound exploration: Discovering stories through listening View activity PDF 2 One Minute of Heritage View activity PDF"},{"u":"chapter-4.html#section-4-case-studies-best-practices","t":"Section 4 · Case studies & best practices","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":""},{"u":"chapter-4.html#successful-case-studies-in-hungary-s4","t":"Successful case studies in Hungary","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Before testing the suggested activities, the following case studies help give useful context. They show how similar activities worked in real situations in the Hungarian background, describing what challenges appeared and what results were achieved. These examples are proof that cultural initiatives funded by Erasmus+ projects can change the reality of many youngsters, discuss real- life problems and become a success on a national and international level."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#case-study-s4-1","t":"4.1. Case study 1 - The voice of youth","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Erasmus+ Youth Journalism Project Source / Reference: See the whole project here Project overview: The Voice of Youth is an Erasmus+ co-funded project involving partners from Hungary, Ireland and Estonia, focused on empowering young people as journalists and media creators . The initiative aims to help young participants build media literacy and content production skills - including writing, blogging , audio /video creation, digital storytelling and critical thinking - so they can express themselves and contribute to community dialogue. Objectives and activities: Equip young people (15–30) with journalistic competencies , including ethical reporting, critical evaluation of media and content design. Develop a multilingual online platform providing interactive lessons, worksheets and evaluation tools to support learning. Facilitate transnational workshops and meet-ups, such as joint sessions in Dublin, where Hungarian participants collaborate with peers from partner countries to review and refine media outputs. Produce co-created content with youth voices embedded, including blog posts, interviews, opinion pieces and journalistic media."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"The project combines media education with experiential learning , allowing young participants to develop journalistic skills through practice. Workshops introduce the fundamentals of journalism , including story selection, ethical reporting, fact-checking and narrative construction. Participants explore different media formats such as blog articles, interviews, podcasts and short videos, learning how to adapt stories to different digital platforms. The methodology is based on participatory learning and collaboration . Young people work in teams to research topics, conduct interviews and produce media content related to social issues, culture and community life. International workshops create opportunities for intercultural exchange , where participants review each other’s work and reflect on different perspectives in storytelling. Digital tools support the learning process through an online platform containing training materials, exercises and evaluation tools. This blended approach - combining workshops, online learning and collaborative production - encourages active engagement and allows participants to learn by doing while developing critical awareness of how media shapes public narratives."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#target-group-s4","t":"Target group","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Young people aged 15–30 interested in media, storytelling and digital content creation Youth with limited access to media literacy education or journalism training Young people motivated to express community issues, social topics or cultural experiences Participants involved in youth organisations, schools or informal learning programmes Youth from different European backgrounds, encouraging intercultural collaboration and exchange"},{"u":"chapter-4.html#key-digital-media-literacy-elements-s4","t":"Key digital & media literacy elements","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Understanding the principles of responsible journalism , including ethical reporting and verification of information Developing critical media literacy , such as analysing bias, credibility and representation in media Learning digital storytelling techniques through blogs, interviews, podcasts and multimedia content Using digital tools and online platforms to publish, share and collaborate on media projects Practising responsible online communication and respectful representation of communities and cultures. Strengthening collaborative production skills, including peer feedback and editorial teamwork"},{"u":"chapter-4.html#impact-s4","t":"Impact","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"The project helped young participants gain confidence in expressing their ideas through media and storytelling. By producing blogs, interviews and multimedia content, they developed practical digital and journalistic skills while reflecting on community issues and cultural perspectives. The project also strengthened collaboration between young people from different countries, encouraging intercultural dialogue and active participation in public discussions. Why it matters: This project demonstrates how media and digital media literacy can be directly incorporated into structured youth work programmes under Erasmus+. By fostering critical thinking alongside practical media skills, The Voice of Youth illustrates how young people gain agency to represent their communities and interpret cultural narratives for broader audiences. The project combines both digital youth work and creative civic participation, aligning closely with the aims of cultural heritage storytelling and youth expression."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#case-study-s4-2","t":"4.2. Case study 2 - Mustár FM: Youth Radio & Audio Media","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Source / Reference: See the whole project here Project overview: Mustár FM is a community youth radio station based in Nyíregyháza, Hungary, that regularly hosts European Solidarity Corps volunteers. Through ESC placements, young people from different European countries are actively involved in audio media production, including radio programmes, interviews, cultural storytelling, and community-oriented broadcasts. The initiative uses radio and audio formats as tools for youth expression, intercultural learning , and civic participation. Objectives and activities: Engage young volunteers in audio content creation , including hosting radio shows, recording interviews, and producing thematic programmes related to culture, language, and youth issues. Develop practical skills in storytelling, communication, and audio production through hands-on participation rather than formal training. Encourage intercultural exchange , with volunteers sharing stories from their countries of origin while engaging with local Hungarian culture and community life. Support youth-led initiatives where volunteers design and deliver their own programme concepts with guidance from local mentors. Activities and outputs: Regular radio broadcasts produced or co-produced by young volunteers. Audio interviews and discussions focusing on cultural identity, European diversity, and local community topics. Informal learning processes that allow volunteers to gradually build confidence in speaking, recording, and storytelling."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4-2","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Mustár FM uses community radio as a practical learning environment where young people develop media skills through active participation. Volunteers take part in producing radio programmes, interviews and cultural storytelling segments, gradually exploring roles such as presenter, interviewer or editor. The methodology is based on informal and experiential learning , where participants learn by doing and reflecting on their work together. Guided by local mentors, volunteers are encouraged to share stories related to culture, identity and community life, while collaborating with peers from different countries and backgrounds."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#target-group-s4-2","t":"Target group","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Young people aged 18–30 participating in European Solidarity Corps placements International volunteers interested in media, storytelling or community engagement Local young people involved in youth radio or community media initiatives Youth seeking informal learning opportunities in communication and digital media Participants interested in intercultural dialogue and cultural exchange"},{"u":"chapter-4.html#key-digital-media-literacy-elements-s4-2","t":"Key digital & media literacy elements","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Learning the basics of audio production and radio broadcasting Developing interview and storytelling skills through real programme creation Practising public speaking and clear communication in audio formats Understanding ethical storytelling and responsible representation in community media Using simple digital recording and editing tools for producing radio content Strengthening collaboration and editorial teamwork in media production Impact: Mustár FM provides young volunteers with hands-on experience in community media production. Through participation in radio programmes and interviews, they develop communication, storytelling and audio production skills while gaining confidence in expressing their perspectives publicly. The project also strengthens intercultural understanding , as volunteers share stories from their countries and engage with local communities through accessible audio content. Why it matters for Ourculture: This case study demonstrates how radio and podcast formats can be successfully integrated into youth work in Hungary. It shows that podcasting does not require high-level technical infrastructure to be impactful; instead, it thrives in environments that prioritise participation, mentorship, and storytelling . For Ourculture, Mustár FM offers a concrete example of how cultural heritage, intercultural narratives, and youth voices can be captured and shared through audio media, aligning strongly with the project’s focus on digital youth work, cultural transmission, and quality innovation in youth engagement."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#case-study-s4-3","t":"4.3. Case study 3 - Diákrádió – Student radio and youth media initiative","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Source / Reference: https://www.radioexpert.net/radio-hungary-live/ Project overview: Diákrádió (Student Radio) initiatives have been implemented in several Hungarian schools and youth organisations to give young people opportunities to experiment with radio production and storytelling . These projects introduce participants to audio media as a tool for communication, creativity and community engagement. Through school or youth-centre-based radio activities, young participants create short programmes, interviews and discussions about topics relevant to their everyday lives, including culture, local traditions and youth perspectives."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#objectives-and-activities-s4","t":"Objectives and activities","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Introduce young people to the basics of radio and podcast production Encourage youth to share stories about local culture, school life and community issues Develop communication and teamwork skills through collaborative media production Provide opportunities for participants to experiment with interviews, audio editing and programme hosting"},{"u":"chapter-4.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4-3","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Student radio initiatives are based on experiential and collaborative learning . Participants explore storytelling by planning and producing short audio segments, interviews and discussions. Activities are guided by youth workers or teachers who support participants in developing programme ideas and organising recordings. The methodology focuses on learning by doing : young people experiment with speaking on air, interviewing others and structuring stories. By working in teams, participants learn how to transform everyday experiences, cultural topics and local stories into engaging audio content ."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#target-group-s4-3","t":"Target group","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Young people aged 14–20 in schools or youth centres Students interested in media, music or storytelling Youth with little or no previous media production experience Participants involved in school clubs or youth programmes Young people interested in expressing their ideas and perspectives"},{"u":"chapter-4.html#key-digital-media-literacy-elements-s4-3","t":"Key digital & media literacy elements","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Introduction to audio storytelling and radio formats Practising interview techniques and public speaking Learning the basics of audio recording and simple editing tools Developing collaboration and editorial teamwork skills Understanding responsible communication and audience awareness"},{"u":"chapter-4.html#impact-s4-2","t":"Impact","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"Student radio initiatives provide young people with a safe space to experiment with media creation and storytelling. Participants gain confidence in expressing their ideas while developing communication and collaboration skills. By discussing topics related to culture, community and everyday life, young people learn how media can be used to share perspectives and strengthen dialogue within their school or local environment."},{"u":"chapter-4.html#why-it-matters-for-ourculture-s4","t":"Why it matters for Ourculture","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":"This case study shows how simple audio media activities can help young people explore and share stories from their everyday environment. By creating radio content about school life, local traditions or community topics, participants learn how media can document cultural experiences and amplify youth voices . For the Ourculture project, it demonstrates how accessible storytelling tools such as student radio can support cultural heritage exploration and digital youth work. ← Back Ch. 3 · Cultural Storytelling Next → Ch. 5 · Digital Citizenship"},{"u":"chapter-4-activity-1.html","t":"Activity 1 · Heritage sound exploration: Discovering stories through listening","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":" Material list 1.The participants will create a collection of raw audio clips per team, and a story map or concept 2.Outline for a heritage-focused mini-episode. Shared insights in group discussion will help 3.Participants to understand the meaning of recording atmospheres and stories. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Reference Speak. Connect. Empower: the role of podcasting in Youth Development Smartphones or audio recorders (one per team minimum) Headphones Notebook and pens Optional: printed site information or historical prompts to spark questions Overview Minimum: exploration + sound collection Recommended: 3 hours with selection and sharing Extended: + a developed audio output Can be split into two sessions. What sounds or voices did I capture that best represent this heritage site? Which story angles emerged - and why do they matter? What was challenging about capturing a story with audio? How might I continue developing your concept into a published episode? Learning objectives A creative, location-based podcasting activity where participants explore a local heritage site together and collect sounds, impressions, and short interviews. Focus is on listening and documenting, rather than producing a finished episode. By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.practise active listening and observation in a cultural context 2.develop narrative ideas rooted in real place-based experience 3.experiment with basic audio recording techniques 4.build confidence in story selection and framing for cultural storytelling. Introduction & warm-up discussion (~30 min) The facilitator introduces podcasting basics: what a story is and how sound can shape it. Later, the group then brainstorms cultural heritage themes and related sounds, drawing on the concepts and examples explored in Chapter 2. Soundwalk Exploration (~60 min) Into 3–4 small teams, participants will choose the sounds they want to record: atmosphere, significant moments, conversations, short interviews with peers or locals… The topic is open to participants. Recommended observation techniques: focus on describing what you see and hear (sounds, people, details, atmosphere) without interpretation or judgement; pay attention to small elements (textures, movements, interactions); take short notes or audio impressions to capture details accurately. Simple guidelines to conduct interviews: preparing a few open questions, creating a respectful atmosphere, listening actively without interrupting and use follow-up questions to deepen answers. Content sharing & story mapping (~60 min) Teams play back selected sound clips and each group sketches a story map: central theme, characters, mood… Each group outlines how their collected material could fit into a short podcast segment. The focus is on ideas, production won’t be prepared. Reflection (~30 min) Participants discuss what surprised them, what problems and reflections emerged, and potential stories worth pursuing. Methodology Steps: Heritage sound exploration: Discovering stories through listening 100 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - hrs "},{"u":"chapter-4-activity-2.html","t":"Activity 2 · One Minute of Heritage","c":"Chapter 4 · Podcasting and blogging","x":" Material list 1.Consciousness of the importance of preparing the subjects. ‘Think, feel, act’. 2.Increase self-confidence in one’s ability to create cultural content. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection smartphones or simple recording devices storyboard created during the previous activity Overview Recommended: 2.5 hours The group reflects on the storytelling experience. Discussion questions: What was the most challenging part of telling a story in one minute for me? Did preparing the storyboard help me in the recording process? How did the short format influence my storytelling choices? Learning objectives In this activity, participants create a one-minute cultural story based on their storyboard. The activity introduces a simple recording method inspired by tourné-monté, where sequences are recorded in the order of the story. Instead of recording large amounts of material and editing later, participants prepare their narrative carefully and record only the sequences that are necessary. This approach encourages intentional storytelling and responsible use of digital tools. By the end of this activity, participants will be able to: 1.create a short cultural story based on a clear narrative intention 2.prepare and record only the elements necessary for the story 3.experiment with simple and intentional media production 4.reflect on how short formats can strengthen storytelling. Preparing the recording (~40 minutes) Participants review their storyboard and identify the sequences that need to be recorded. Recording the story (~1h20) Participants record their sequences in the order of the story. They focus on capturing only the essential moments needed to communicate their narrative. Sharing the stories (~30 minutes) Participants present their one-minute stories to the group. Methodology Steps: One Minute of Heritage 101 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - Continue after the workshop A first production can become the starting point for a blog article, a mini podcast series, a collection of local sounds or a digital exhibition. 2.5 hrs Record less, think more "},{"u":"chapter-5.html#infographic-summary","t":"I N F O G R A P H I C S U M M A R Y","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Visual summary of this chapter — click to enlarge or download. Download infographic (PDF)"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#intro","t":"Introduction","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital citizenship refers to the ways individuals participate, interact and contribute responsibly in digital environments. For young people growing up in highly connected societies, digital spaces are not separate from civic life; they are central arenas where identities are expressed, communities are formed and social debates unfold. This chapter approaches digital citizenship as an active, ethical and participatory practice , rather than a set of technical rules or behavioural restrictions. It explores how young people can understand their rights and responsibilities online , engage respectfully with diverse perspectives and contribute positively to digital communities. Particular attention is given to key themes such as cyber-ethics, digital inclusion, respectful communication and civic participation in online spaces. Within the OURCULTURE progression, this chapter builds on the earlier stages of collecting cultural material, developing narratives and producing media, and focuses on how these outputs can be shared responsibly and meaningfully in digital spaces. By combining conceptual explanations with practical tools, youth workers are supported in guiding young people from simple digital expression toward meaningful civic and cultural engagement. This approach is aligned with key European frameworks such as the European Digital Competence Framework (DigComp) , the EU Youth Strategy and the EntreComp framework, which emphasise responsible participation, critical thinking and initiative in digital and civic life."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#learning-objectives-sintro","t":"Learning objectives","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"By the end of this chapter, youth workers will be able to: explain digital citizenship as a lived practice rather than a set of rules support young people in understanding online rights and responsibilities facilitate ethical, inclusive and safe digital participation use project-based activities to connect digital tools with civic engagement draw inspiration from European and local good practices . These activities also encourage initiative and project-based thinking, helping participants see how responsible digital participation can lead to the creation of meaningful digital initiatives or community projects. Sections Section 1 | Understanding digital citizenship Section 2 | Cyber-ethics, inclusion and digital participation Section 3 | Practical activities for youth work Section 4 | Case studies & best practices"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#section-1-understanding-digital-citizenship","t":"Section 1 · Understanding digital citizenship","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":""},{"u":"chapter-5.html#online-rights-and-responsibilities-s1","t":"1.1. Online rights and responsibilities","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital citizenship refers to the ability to participate in digital environments ethically, responsibly and actively , while recognising both individual rights and shared responsibilities . In contemporary societies, digital spaces are not separate from everyday life; they function as public arenas where opinions are expressed, identities are shaped and social relationships are negotiated. For young people, digital citizenship is therefore not limited to knowing platform rules or technical functions. It involves understanding how digital actions affect oneself and others, recognising boundaries and making informed decisions in environments that are often fast-paced, emotional and highly visible. Digital citizens are not passive users of technology . They actively participate in digital environments as communicators and contributors to online communities. As explored in the previous chapters of this guide, young people may also act as content creators and storytellers when documenting and sharing cultural experiences. In this chapter, the focus shifts toward how these roles are exercised responsibly. It emphasises how young people engage with others, make ethical decisions and contribute to digital spaces in ways that respect rights, inclusion and collective well-being. Rather than redefining digital participation , this chapter builds on earlier competences and focuses specifically on their ethical and civic implications ."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#online-rights-s1","t":"1.2. Online rights","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Online rights provide the foundation for meaningful and democratic participation in digital environments. These rights are closely linked to fundamental human rights, but they take on new forms and challenges in digital spaces."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#right-to-privacy-and-data-protection-s1","t":"Right to privacy and data protection","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Young people have the right to control their personal data and understand how it is collected, stored and shared. This includes awareness of digital footprints and the long-term implications of online visibility. Privacy should not be understood as secrecy or withdrawal, but as the ability to make informed choices about personal information and digital identity."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#right-to-freedom-of-expression-s1","t":"Right to freedom of expression","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital environments offer young people important opportunities to express opinions, identities and cultural perspectives. Freedom of expression supports creativity, dialogue and democratic participation. At the same time, this right exists within legal and ethical boundaries , ensuring that expression does not harm others through harassment, discrimination or hate speech."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#right-to-access-information-s1","t":"Right to access information","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Access to reliable and diverse information is essential for informed participation in society. Digital citizenship therefore includes the right to seek, receive and share information without unnecessary barriers, manipulation or discrimination. Supporting this right also requires the development of media literacy skills , allowing young people to evaluate sources and recognise misinformation."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#right-to-protection-from-harm-s1","t":"Right to protection from harm","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Young people have the right to protection from online harassment , hate speech, exploitation, manipulation and other forms of digital harm. This includes access to reporting mechanisms, support networks and digital environments that encourage safe and respectful participation."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#online-responsibilities-s1","t":"1.3. Online responsibilities","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital citizenship also involves responsibilities that accompany these rights. Responsible behaviour online should not be framed as restriction or control, but as a way to maintain fair, inclusive and respectful digital communities ."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#respect-for-others-rights-and-dignity-s1","t":"Respect for others’ rights and dignity","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital spaces are shared spaces . Respecting diversity, avoiding harassment and recognising different identities, cultures and perspectives are fundamental elements of responsible participation."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#responsible-content-creation-and-sharing-s1","t":"Responsible content creation and sharing","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Before publishing or sharing content, young people should consider consent, context and potential impact ."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#think-about-it-s1","t":"Think about it","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"A friend posts a photo of you online without asking. What can you do? What should they have done before posting it? This is particularly important when content involves other individuals, cultural practices or sensitive topics. Ethical content creation helps maintain trust and respect in digital communities."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#critical-engagement-with-information-s1","t":"Critical engagement with information","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital citizens have a responsibility to question sources, recognise misinformation and avoid spreading unverified or harmful content. Sharing information is not a neutral action ; it is a form of participation that can influence public understanding and social dialogue."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#awareness-of-consequences-s1","t":"Awareness of consequences","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital actions can have long-term consequences . Comments, images and posts may circulate far beyond their original context and remain accessible for extended periods. Understanding this permanence supports more conscious, reflective and ethical decision-making in digital environments. When publishing content such as a video, podcast or blog, these consequences may extend beyond the immediate audience and remain visible over time."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#implications-for-youth-work-s1","t":"1.4. Implications for youth work","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"In youth work contexts, promoting digital citizenship means creating spaces where young people can explore rights and responsibilities through real-life digital situations , rather than memorising abstract rules. For example, youth workers may facilitate discussions around situations such as: a photo from a youth activity being shared online without the consent of everyone in the image conflicts or exclusion occurring in group chats or online communities the spread of misleading information within peer networks disagreements about freedom of expression and respectful communication Discussing these situations helps young people understand how digital rights and responsibilities interact in everyday practice. Effective youth work practice: uses concrete digital scenarios and dilemmas encourages dialogue rather than judgement connects rights and responsibilities to everyday online experiences treats mistakes as learning opportunities within safe environments supports young people in reflecting on the impact of their digital actions on others By integrating digital citizenship into creative, cultural and participatory activities, youth work helps young people develop confidence, ethical awareness and agency in digital environments. This approach also connects naturally with the OURCULTURE methodology, where digital participation supports storytelling, cultural exploration and youth-led narratives. Key message Digital citizenship is not about limiting young people’s online presence. It is about enabling them to participate consciously, responsibly and confidently . When young people understand both their rights and their responsibilities , digital spaces can become places of dialogue, expression and shared responsibility – rather than conflict, fear or exclusion."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#section-2-cyber-ethics-inclusion-and-digital-participation","t":"Section 2 · Cyber-ethics, inclusion and digital participation","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital environments are not neutral spaces . They reflect social norms, cultural hierarchies, economic interests and power relations that influence who is visible, whose voices are amplified and who remains unheard. For this reason, digital citizenship cannot be understood separately from questions of ethics, inclusion and participation . In youth work contexts, these three dimensions are closely interconnected. Ethical behaviour helps create inclusive environments; inclusive environments enable participation; and meaningful participation strengthens ethical awareness."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#cyber-ethics-acting-with-awareness-in-digital-spaces-s2","t":"2.1. Cyber-Ethics: Acting with awareness in digital spaces","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Cyber-ethics refers to the ethical principles that guide behaviour in digital environments. Rather than abstract moral rules, cyber-ethics concerns the everyday decisions people make when they communicate, share, comment or create content online. Ethical challenges often arise in ordinary situations, such as: sharing photos or videos of others commenting on sensitive or controversial topics representing other people’s stories or experiences responding to disagreement or online conflict deciding what deserves attention or visibility Developing cyber-ethical awareness helps young people recognise that digital actions always have social consequences . Key principles of cyber-ethics include:"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#consent-and-representation-s2","t":"Consent and representation","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Ethical digital practice requires informed and ongoing consent before recording, sharing or reusing images, voices or personal stories. In youth and cultural projects, this also means respecting how individuals and communities wish to be represented , not only whether they agree to appear in content. For example, when publishing a video, podcast or blog post created during a youth activity, it is important to ensure that all participants have given informed consent and feel comfortable with how they are represented."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#respectful-communication-s2","t":"Respectful communication","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital participation should encourage dialogue rather than confrontation . Cyber-ethics invites young people to express disagreement without humiliation, harassment or exclusion, recognising that tone and language shape the culture of online spaces . This becomes especially relevant when moderating comments under a video, responding to feedback on a blog post or engaging with audiences after publishing digital content."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#power-visibility-and-algorithms-s2","t":"Power, visibility and algorithms","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Not all voices receive equal visibility online . Algorithms, popularity metrics and platform design influence what becomes visible and what remains hidden. For example, recommendation systems on social media platforms often prioritise content that generates strong reactions such as anger, excitement or controversy. As a result, emotionally charged or polarising content may spread more widely than balanced or nuanced information. In other cases, algorithms may amplify already popular voices while less visible perspectives remain marginalised. Developing ethical awareness includes recognising how these systems shape digital attention. Young people benefit from understanding that what they see online is not neutral or random , but influenced by platform design and engagement metrics. Questioning visibility, asking whose voices are missing and reflecting on why certain content spreads quickly are important elements of responsible digital citizenship."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#accountability-and-responsibility-s2","t":"Accountability and responsibility","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Ethical participation involves taking responsibility for one’s actions online . This includes acknowledging mistakes, correcting misinformation and responding constructively to feedback rather than denying or escalating conflict."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#practical-facilitation-considerations-s2","t":"Practical facilitation considerations","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"In youth work settings, ethical conflicts in digital spaces may occasionally arise during activities or online interactions. Youth workers play an important role in responding constructively and modelling responsible digital behaviour . Possible facilitation responses may include: encouraging participants to pause and reflect before reacting to online conflict supporting respectful dialogue between participants when misunderstandings occur addressing exclusionary behaviour in group chats or online discussions clarifying community rules and digital participation agreements temporarily moderating discussions or removing harmful content if necessary These responses should prioritise dialogue, learning and restoration rather than punishment . When young people experience how conflicts can be addressed respectfully, they develop stronger awareness of accountability and responsible digital participation."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#inclusion-beyond-access-to-meaningful-participation-s2","t":"2.2. Inclusion: Beyond access to meaningful participation","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"In digital environments, inclusion is often reduced to access to devices or internet connectivity. However, access alone does not guarantee participation . The real barriers to participation frequently relate to confidence, representation and perceived legitimacy . Young people may remain silent not because they lack skills, but because they: fear judgement or exposure do not see people like themselves represented feel their voice is not valued associate digital spaces with conflict or harassment Inclusive digital participation therefore requires attention to the conditions that enable young people to contribute safely and confidently . Key dimensions of inclusion include:"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#diversity-of-identities-and-experiences-s2","t":"Diversity of identities and experiences","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital spaces should welcome different cultural backgrounds, languages, abilities and life experiences. Recognising diverse perspectives helps prevent marginalisation and avoids reinforcing dominant narratives ."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#unequal-conditions-of-access-and-familiarity-s2","t":"Unequal conditions of access and familiarity","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Young people’s access to devices, connectivity and digital confidence varies widely. Inclusive youth work practices favour low-threshold tools, flexible formats and collaborative learning environments."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#psychological-safety-s2","t":"Psychological safety","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Participation grows where young people feel protected from ridicule, shaming or exclusion . Clear rules, supportive facilitation and respectful dialogue are essential for creating environments where participants feel safe to express themselves."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#legitimacy-of-voice-s2","t":"Legitimacy of voice","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Inclusive environments recognise that every participant has something meaningful to express , regardless of technical skills, popularity or prior experience. Youth work plays an essential role in actively creating these conditions, rather than assuming they will emerge automatically."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#digital-participation-from-expression-to-civic-and-cultural-s2","t":"2.3. Digital participation: From expression to civic and cultural engagement","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital participation goes beyond simply posting content online. It involves engaging with others, contributing to shared narratives and taking part in public dialogue. For instance, when sharing a cultural story through a video, podcast or social media post, participation does not end with publishing . It continues through dialogue, feedback and interaction with audiences. It is useful to distinguish between digital expression and digital participation . Digital expression refers to individual actions such as posting content, sharing opinions, commenting or reacting online. While these actions allow young people to express themselves, they do not always involve interaction or collective processes. Digital participation, on the other hand, involves engaging with others, contributing to discussions, collaborating across perspectives and participating in shared decision-making or community dialogue. Participation therefore moves beyond individual visibility and becomes a form of civic and cultural engagement. Meaningful digital participation may include: sharing stories rooted in lived experiences engaging respectfully in discussions on social or cultural issues collaborating across differences and perspectives connecting local voices to broader communities When participation is supported by ethical awareness and inclusive environments, digital spaces can become learning environments where young people develop agency rather than simply visibility . For youth professionals, supporting digital participation involves: modelling ethical and inclusive digital behaviour facilitating respectful dialogue and mediating conflicts encouraging reflection on participation experiences connecting digital expression to broader cultural and civic contexts Digital participation also opens opportunities for young people to develop projects that create social or cultural value . When young people design online campaigns, community platforms or digital storytelling initiatives, they combine civic participation with entrepreneurial competences such as initiative, collaboration and project development. In this sense, digital citizenship can also be understood as the ability to shape digital environments responsibly while turning ideas into meaningful action . This approach also aligns with the OURCULTURE methodology, where digital tools support storytelling, cultural exploration and youth-led narratives."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#initiative-and-responsible-digital-innovation-s2","t":"2.4. Initiative and responsible digital innovation","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Digital citizenship does not only concern behaviour in existing online spaces; it also relates to how new digital initiatives are created. Young people today increasingly act as designers of digital environments through blogs, platforms, campaigns or collaborative online projects. Developing initiative and project- thinking helps young people move from passive participation to active contribution . These competences include identifying needs in communities, imagining solutions and organising collaborative efforts to address them. Entrepreneurial frameworks such as the European Commission’s EntreComp model describe entrepreneurship as the ability to transform ideas into action through creativity, initiative and collaboration. In youth work contexts, these competences can support young people in designing responsible digital projects that promote inclusion , dialogue and cultural expression. When combined with ethical awareness, such initiatives help ensure that digital innovation strengthens democratic participation rather than reinforcing exclusion or misinformation. When developing digital projects such as blogs, platforms or storytelling campaigns, these ethical and participatory considerations become part of the design process itself . This perspective is closely connected to the EntreComp framework, which defines entrepreneurship as the ability to transform ideas into action through creativity, initiative and responsibility."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#why-this-matters-for-youth-work-s2","t":"2.5. Why this matters for youth work","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Cyber-ethics , inclusion and participation should not be treated as separate learning topics. Together, they shape how young people experience digital environments – and whether these environments become empowering spaces for dialogue or sources of exclusion and conflict. Effective youth work practice therefore: integrates ethical reflection into creative activities prioritises inclusion by design rather than as an afterthought treats participation as a learning process rather than a performance supports youth workers as facilitators rather than controllers These dimensions also reflect priorities of the EU Youth Strategy , which promotes youth participation, inclusion and active citizenship in both digital and physical spaces. Key message Digital participation without ethics can cause harm. Ethics without participation remain abstract. Inclusion without voice is incomplete. When cyber-ethics, inclusion and digital participation are addressed together, digital spaces can become environments of dialogue, cultural expression and democratic engagement – where young people are not only present, but genuinely involved."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#section-3-practical-activities-for-youth-work","t":"Section 3 · Practical activities for youth work","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"1 Mapping my digital citizenship View activity PDF 2 Design your digital start-up View activity PDF 3 Digital dilemmas lab View activity PDF 4 Inclusive digital participation lab View activity PDF 5 Community opportunity lab View activity PDF 6 Entrepreneurial pitch for cultural projects View activity PDF"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#section-4-case-studies-best-practices","t":"Section 4 · Case studies & best practices","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":""},{"u":"chapter-5.html#case-study-s4-1","t":"4.1. Case study 1 - Civil society–led critical digital literacy initiative (Türkiye)","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Source / Reference: Dijital Esenlik – Critical Digital Literacy and Digital Wellbeing Initiative, Türkiye | https://dijitalesenlik.org Project overview: Dijital Esenlik is an independent, non-governmental initiative based in Istanbul that works on critical digital literacy, information integrity and responsible technology . Operating outside both state structures and commercial technology platforms, it supports individuals, communities and civil society organisations in moving from a passive relationship with digital technologies towards active, informed and critical participation in digital life. The initiative approaches digital literacy as more than a set of technical skills. Its work starts from the structural conditions that shape digital environments – algorithmic systems, attention economies, digital rights and unequal access – and creates facilitated spaces in which participants can examine these conditions collectively and imagine alternatives. This structural perspective complements skills-based approaches by encouraging young people to question not only individual pieces of content, but also the systems and institutions that produce and distribute information."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#objectives-and-learning-focus-s4","t":"Objectives and learning focus","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"The initiative aims to: strengthen critical digital literacy beyond formal education settings raise awareness of algorithmic systems, digital rights and information integrity encourage critical reflection on the structural dimensions of digital environments build the digital capacity of civil society organisations and community actors support participants in imagining and articulating alternative digital futures Rather than transmitting fixed answers, the programmes are designed to develop participants’ capacity to ask informed questions about the digital systems they use every day."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"The initiative works primarily through facilitated workshops and programme design, combining critical inquiry with participatory and creative methods . Learning is structured around collective discussion and reflection rather than lecture-based instruction. Key elements of the methodology include: facilitated workshops on algorithms, digital rights and our relationship with technology critical thinking exercises applied to everyday digital experiences collective imagination and foresight methods, such as the Futures Cone , used to explore alternative digital futures field research feeding back into programme and institutional design capacity-building support for civil society organisations on digital tools, communication and design Facilitators come from a range of professional backgrounds – journalism, civil society, psychology, communications and academia – which allows digital literacy to be approached from social, ethical and psychological perspectives at the same time."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#examples-of-programmes-s4","t":"Examples of programmes","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":""},{"u":"chapter-5.html#baska-bir-dijital-mumkun-mu-is-another-digital-possible-s4","t":"“Başka Bir Dijital Mümkün Mü?” (Is another digital possible?)","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Workshops with university students, educators and facilitators working with young people, exploring algorithms, digital rights and the structural problems of the digital world through critical thinking and collective imagination exercises. Implemented in Istanbul and Ankara in partnership with Kreakt."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#55-dijital-esenlik-s4","t":"55+ Dijital Esenlik","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"A workshop series and community programme supporting adults aged 55 and over in building independent and secure digital capacity, framing digital access as a question of rights rather than individual ability . Supported by Roof, Postane and the Borusan Sustainable Impact Programme."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#target-group-s4","t":"Target group","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"young people and university students educators, youth workers and facilitators working with young people civil society organisations seeking to strengthen their digital capacity older adults (55+) and professionals working with them communities with limited access to structured critical digital education"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#key-digital-media-literacy-elements-s4","t":"Key digital & media literacy elements","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"algorithmic awareness and understanding of platform logics information integrity and critical evaluation of digital content digital rights , access and inclusion as structural questions critical reflection on institutions and systems that produce information digital wellbeing and conscious management of attention"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#impact-s4","t":"Impact","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Civil society–led initiatives of this kind contribute to: extending critical digital literacy to groups not reached by formal or institutional programmes introducing structural and rights-based perspectives alongside individual skills training strengthening the digital capacity of the wider civil society sector creating spaces where young people can question digital systems rather than only adapt to them Because the initiative is independent of both public institutions and commercial platforms , it is able to examine the role of those actors critically – an important complement to media literacy programmes delivered by media organisations or public bodies themselves."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#what-youth-workers-can-take-from-this-example-s4","t":"What youth workers can take from this example","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Youth workers can extend media literacy beyond content analysis by helping young people examine the systems behind the content : who designs the platforms they use, whose interests those designs serve, and what alternatives might be possible. Facilitation-based and foresight methods , such as collective imagination exercises, offer accessible ways to do this in non-formal settings. This example also shows the value of applying critical thinking to institutions themselves – including those that provide media and digital training – and of partnering with independent civil society actors who can bring that perspective into youth work."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#case-study-s4-2","t":"4.2. Case study 2 - Decidim: Digital democracy platform","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Source / Reference: Decidim – Digital Democracy Platform https://decidim.org Project overview: Decidim is an open-source digital participation platform originally developed in Barcelona to support democratic decision-making and citizen participation. The platform enables communities, municipalities and civil society organisations to organise consultations, discussions and collaborative decision-making processes online. Through Decidim, citizens – including young people – can propose ideas, debate policies, vote on initiatives and contribute to public decision-making processes."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#objectives-and-learning-focus-s4-2","t":"Objectives and learning focus","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"The initiative aims to: strengthen democratic participation in digital environments empower citizens to engage in collective decision-making increase transparency and accountability in public governance encourage inclusive participation in civic processes For young people, the platform provides an opportunity to experience digital citizenship as active democratic participation ."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4-2","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Decidim combines digital tools with participatory governance practices . Key elements include: online consultations and debates collaborative proposal systems participatory budgeting processes transparent decision-making mechanisms Participants can submit proposals, discuss ideas and track how decisions evolve. The platform emphasises transparency, allowing users to follow the impact of their contributions ."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#target-group-s4-2","t":"Target group","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"citizens and community members young people interested in civic participation civil society organisations municipalities and local governments"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#key-digital-citizenship-elements-s4","t":"Key digital citizenship elements","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"democratic participation in digital environments collective decision-making and dialogue transparency and accountability inclusive civic engagement"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#impact-s4-2","t":"Impact","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Decidim has been adopted by numerous municipalities and organisations across Europe and beyond. The platform has enabled thousands of citizens to participate in democratic processes and contribute ideas to public decision-making. For youth work contexts, Decidim demonstrates how digital platforms can support active digital citizenship and participatory democracy ."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#case-study-s4-3","t":"4.3. Case study 3 - European Youth Dialogue","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Source / Reference: European Youth Dialogue https://youth.europa.eu/eu-youth-dialogue_en"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#project-overview-s4","t":"Project overview","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"The European Youth Dialogue is a participatory process connecting young people with policymakers across Europe. Through consultations, workshops and digital platforms, young people share their perspectives on social issues and contribute to shaping youth policies at national and European levels. Digital tools play an important role in enabling large-scale youth participation and ensuring that diverse voices are heard ."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#objectives-and-learning-focus-s4-3","t":"Objectives and learning focus","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"The initiative aims to: strengthen youth participation in democratic decision-making connect young people with policy processes promote dialogue between youth and decision-makers support inclusive and diverse youth representation"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4-3","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"The European Youth Dialogue operates through a cycle of consultations and participatory events involving young people from different countries. Key activities include: online consultations and surveys youth workshops and conferences digital platforms for collecting ideas and feedback collaborative discussions between youth representatives and policymakers These processes allow young people to share experiences and contribute to policy discussions."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#target-group-s4-3","t":"Target group","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"young people aged 13–30 across Europe youth organisations and youth workers policymakers and institutions involved in youth policy"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#key-digital-citizenship-elements-s4-2","t":"Key digital citizenship elements","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"democratic participation and civic engagement dialogue between citizens and institutions representation of diverse youth voices collaborative policy development"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#impact-s4-3","t":"Impact","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"The European Youth Dialogue has influenced several policy recommendations within the EU Youth Strategy. It has also strengthened awareness among young people that their voices can contribute to shaping policies and social priorities. The initiative demonstrates how digital participation can connect local youth perspectives with broader European decision-making processes."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#case-study-s4-4","t":"4.4. Case study 4 - Youth entrepreneurship programme","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"(GINOP & VEKOP) Source / Reference: See the whole project here https://betterentrepreneurship.eu/en/case-study/youth-entrepreneurship- programme-ginop-and-vekop-hungary Project overview: The Youth Entrepreneurship Programme (GINOP and VEKOP) is a national initiative launched in Hungary in 2014 to encourage young people to create their own businesses. The programme provides entrepreneurship training, mentoring, and financial support for start-ups. It targets young people aged 18–30 (and in some cases up to 35), with a particular focus on unemployed youth and individuals interested in developing their own business ideas. The initiative operates through regional programmes across Hungary, allowing training content and support mechanisms to be adapted to local labour market conditions . It combines education with practical support such as business planning workshops and start-up funding schemes. Objectives and activities: The programme focuses on developing entrepreneurial competences and helping participants turn business ideas into viable enterprises . Activities include: Entrepreneurship training courses focused on business planning and financial management Mentoring and coaching by experienced professionals Support in developing business plans Access to start-up financing for selected projects Participants work through structured training sessions where they learn how to analyse markets, develop products or services, and build sustainable business models."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4-4","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"The programme combines structured training with practical mentoring to help young participants develop entrepreneurial competences. Participants attend workshops on business planning, financial management and market analysis, while also receiving guidance from experienced mentors. The approach is based on learning through project development , where participants gradually transform business ideas into concrete plans. Alongside training sessions, participants receive feedback on their ideas and explore how to build sustainable initiatives. This combination of education, mentoring and financial support helps young people move from initial ideas to viable entrepreneurial projects . Digital tools are also used during the training process, for example through online collaboration platforms, digital presentations and virtual mentoring sessions, allowing participants to develop communication and project development skills in digital environments."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#target-group-s4-4","t":"Target group","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Young people aged 18–30 (up to 35 in some programmes) Unemployed youth seeking new career opportunities Young people interested in starting their own business or initiative Participants with early-stage business ideas or project concepts Young adults looking to develop entrepreneurial and project management skills"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#key-digital-citizenship-elements-s4-3","t":"Key digital citizenship elements","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"The programme encourages responsible participation in digital economic environments . Participants learn how to use digital platforms to communicate ideas, promote projects and collaborate with partners. Through activities such as online research, digital presentations and communication with mentors, young people develop awareness of responsible digital behaviour, credibility of information and ethical representation of initiatives . These experiences strengthen their ability to participate confidently and responsibly in digital professional and entrepreneurial spaces."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#impact-s4-4","t":"Impact","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"The programme has supported thousands of young people in developing entrepreneurial skills and launching new initiatives across Hungary. Participants gain practical experience in transforming ideas into structured projects while building confidence, leadership and problem-solving abilities. By combining training, mentoring and financial support, the initiative reduces barriers to entrepreneurship and encourages young people to explore innovative solutions for economic and social challenges."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#connection-to-youth-work-practice-s4","t":"Connection to youth work practice","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"For youth work contexts, the programme demonstrates how entrepreneurial learning can empower young people to transform ideas into real initiatives . Youth workers can adapt similar approaches by supporting participants in identifying opportunities in their communities, developing project ideas and presenting them to others. Integrating entrepreneurial thinking into youth activities helps strengthen initiative, problem-solving and collaboration, while encouraging young people to actively shape social, cultural or digital projects within their communities."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#case-study-s4-5","t":"4.5. Case study 5 - Design Terminal startup incubation and innovation","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Source / Reference: Design Terminal, which became Civitta Hungary in 2024: https://civitta.com/ Project overview: Design Terminal is a Budapest-based innovation agency and incubator that supports startups, entrepreneurs, and innovation projects across Central Europe. Originally established as a government initiative and later transformed into a social enterprise , Design Terminal works with startups, corporations, and educational institutions to foster innovation and entrepreneurship. The organisation runs incubation programmes, mentoring schemes, and innovation challenges aimed at helping entrepreneurs develop scalable businesses. It also collaborates with major companies and international networks to connect Hungarian startups with global markets. Objectives and activities: Design Terminal focuses on strengthening entrepreneurial ecosystems through practical support and innovation programmes. Its activities include: Startup incubation programmes supporting early-stage entrepreneurs Mentoring and coaching from experienced entrepreneurs and industry experts Innovation labs and hackathons addressing social or technological challenges Partnerships with universities and corporations to support startup development"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#content-and-methodological-approach-s4-5","t":"Content and methodological approach","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Design Terminal uses an innovation-driven learning approach combining incubation programmes, mentoring and collaborative experimentation. Participants engage in workshops, innovation labs and hackathons where they develop ideas, test solutions and refine business concepts with the support of mentors and industry experts. The methodology emphasises learning through practice, teamwork and feedback . By connecting startups with experienced professionals, corporate partners and international networks, participants gain practical insights into innovation processes and entrepreneurship while developing their own projects in real-world contexts . These activities often rely on digital collaboration tools, online prototyping platforms and virtual communication channels, enabling teams to co-create solutions, present ideas and connect with international innovation networks."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#target-group-s4-5","t":"Target group","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Early-stage entrepreneurs and startup founders Young professionals interested in innovation and entrepreneurship University students developing technology or business ideas Teams participating in innovation challenges or hackathons Young innovators seeking mentorship and international networks"},{"u":"chapter-5.html#key-digital-citizenship-elements-s4-4","t":"Key digital citizenship elements","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Participants develop awareness of responsible innovation and the social impact of digital technologies. Through collaborative projects and innovation challenges, they reflect on how digital tools influence communities, users and markets. The programmes encourage transparency, ethical decision-making and responsible communication when developing and presenting digital products or services. These experiences help participants understand that digital innovation also requires accountability and awareness of its broader social implications."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#impact-s4-5","t":"Impact","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"Design Terminal has contributed significantly to the development of Hungary’s startup ecosystem by supporting entrepreneurs in transforming innovative ideas into viable projects. Through incubation programmes and mentoring schemes, participants gain practical experience, professional networks and strategic guidance. The initiative encourages experimentation and collaboration, helping young innovators develop solutions to social, technological and economic challenges while strengthening the innovation culture in the region."},{"u":"chapter-5.html#connection-to-youth-work-practice-s4-2","t":"Connection to youth work practice","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":"For youth work practice, Design Terminal demonstrates how innovation- based learning can inspire young people to develop creative solutions to real challenges. Youth workers can adapt similar approaches by organising idea labs, collaborative workshops or small innovation challenges where participants explore community issues and design projects together. Such activities strengthen initiative, teamwork and creative thinking while encouraging young people to actively shape digital, social or cultural initiatives in their communities. ← Back Ch. 4 · Podcasting and blogging Next → Conclusion"},{"u":"chapter-5-activity-1.html","t":"Activity 1 · Mapping my digital citizenship","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":" Material list Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection 1.Personal digital citizenship maps created by participants 2.Increased awareness of how rights, responsibilities and participation interact online 3.Shared understanding of digital citizenship as a lived experience 4.Reflection on inclusion, ethics and participation in digital environments Reference Digital citizenship education approaches used in non-formal youth work, combined with media literacy and ethical participation frameworks commonly applied in European youth programmes. A4 papers or large sheets Coloured markers or pens Sticky notes Flipchart or wall space Overview Minimum: 45 minutes Extended: 90 minutes with deeper discussion Use orally or in writing: One digital habit I want to rethink is… One right I want to protect more is… One responsibility I sometimes forget is… One way digital spaces could be more inclusive is… Learning objectives This activity introduces digital citizenship through personal reflection and collective dialogue. Instead of starting with theoretical definitions, participants explore their own digital behaviours, rights, responsibilities and forms of participation. Through a simple visual mapping exercise, young people recognise that digital citizenship is not an abstract concept but a lived everyday practice shaped by their own experiences online. The activity encourages participants to reflect on how their digital actions influence others and how online environments can become more inclusive, respectful and participatory. By the end of the activity, participants will be able to: 1.understand digital citizenship as a balance between rights, responsibilities and participation 2.reflect on their own digital behaviours and online habits 3.recognise how individual digital actions affect others 4.identify areas where they feel confident, unsure or excluded online. Warm-up discussion (~15 min) The facilitator opens with simple, relatable questions: Where do you spend most of your time online? When do you feel comfortable online? When do you feel uncomfortable or silent? Participants share voluntarily. No evaluation or correction is made. Personal digital citizenship map (~25 min) Each participant creates a simple map divided into four sections: 1. My rights online (e.g. expression, privacy, access to information) 2. My responsibilities online (e.g. respect, consent, sharing carefully) 3. My risks & concerns (e.g. harassment, privacy, misinformation) 4. My ways of participating (e.g. posting, commenting, creating, observing) Participants write or draw freely. There is no “right” answer. Small group sharing (~30 min) Participants form small groups (3–4 people) and discuss: Similarities and differences between maps Areas where many people feel unsure or silent Situations where rights and responsibilities overlap or conflict The facilitator circulates, listening and supporting dialogue. Collective reflection (~20 min) In plenary, the group reflects together: Which digital rights are most important to us? Which responsibilities are most challenging? What makes participation easier or harder? How can digital spaces become more inclusive? The facilitator connects reflections to key digital citizenship concepts without lecturing. Methodology Steps: Mapping my digital citizenship 129 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 90 min. "},{"u":"chapter-5-activity-2.html","t":"Activity 2 · Design your digital start-up","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":" Material list 1.Digital start-up concepts grounded in ethical and inclusive thinking 2.Increased awareness of responsibility in digital innovation 3.Stronger link between digital creativity and civic values Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Reference Non-formal entrepreneurship education, digital citizenship frameworks and ethical innovation approaches commonly used in youth work and youth entrepreneurship programmes across Europe. Flipchart papers or large sheets Markers and sticky notes “Start-up Canvas” template (can be simple hand-drawn) Timer or clock Overview Minimum: 90 minutes Recommended: 2 hours What responsibility does a digital creator have toward users? How can design choices encourage respectful participation? What risks should digital innovators always consider? How would you change your idea if real users were involved? Learning objectives This activity invites young people to design a fictional digital start-up while applying digital citizenship principles. Participants explore how digital platforms shape rights, inclusion, ethics and participation, and how technology influences behaviours and relationships. It encourages them to turn these ideas into concrete design choices, reflecting on the social impact of innovation and their responsibility as digital creators to build inclusive and ethical environments. By the end of the activity, participants will be able to: 1.understand how digital products influence users’ rights and responsibilities 2.apply ethical thinking to digital innovation and platform design 3.recognise inclusion, accessibility and safety as essential design principles 4.reflect on the social and civic impact of digital tools 5.connect digital creativity with responsible digital citizenship 6.reflect on ethical responsibilities in digital innovation 7.understand how digital products shape participation and inclusion. Introduction: Digital products shape society (~15 min) The facilitator introduces the idea with guiding questions: Do digital platforms only offer services, or do they shape behaviour? Who benefits most from digital tools? Who might be excluded or harmed? Participants are reminded that every digital product carries values, whether intentional or not Group formation & start-up brief (~15 min) Participants form small teams (3–5 people) and are tasked with designing a digital start-up that addresses a real social, cultural or youth-related need. Examples include platforms for youth voices, cultural storytelling apps, safe discussion spaces or learning and wellbeing tools. Digital start-up design canvas (~45 min) Teams develop their idea using the following guiding sections: 1. Problem & need What issue does the start-up address? Who experiences this problem? 2. Target group Who are the users? Who might be unintentionally excluded? 3. Digital rights How are users’ rights protected (privacy, expression, access)? What data is collected and why? 4. Ethics & responsibility How does the platform prevent harm, misinformation or abuse? How is consent handled? 5. Inclusion & participation How can different voices participate? How does the platform support safe engagement? 6. Positive impact How does this start-up contribute to society or culture? Facilitators circulate, asking reflective questions rather than giving answers. Start-up pitches (~25 min) Each group presents their start-up in 3–5 minutes, focusing on: The idea, Ethical choices, Inclusion strategies, Digital citizenship elements, Other participants listen and note: One strong ethical decision, One inclusion feature they appreciate Reflection & Debrief (~20 min) Group discussion guided by questions: Which design decisions were hardest? Where did rights and business ideas conflict? How did inclusion change the design? What surprised you about designing ethically? The facilitator links insights back to digital citizenship principles. Methodology Steps: Design your digital start-up 130 . 5 2 hrs "},{"u":"chapter-5-activity-3.html","t":"Activity 3 · Digital dilemmas lab","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":" Material list 1.Increased ethical awareness in digital contexts 2.Improved ability to articulate and justify decisions 3.Stronger understanding of inclusion and responsibility 4.Experience in respectful disagreement and dialogue Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Reference Ethics-based youth work methodologies, digital citizenship education, dilemma- based learning in non-formal education. Printed or written dilemma cards Flipchart paper Markers Sticky notes Overview Minimum: 60 minutes with fewer dilemmas Recommended: 70 minutes Extended: 90 minutes Was it easy or difficult to decide what is “right”? How did other people’s perspectives change your thinking? How can we apply this kind of reflection before acting online? What responsibilities do we have even when platforms allow harmful behaviour? Learning objectives This activity places young people in realistic digital dilemma scenarios where there is no single “correct” answer. Participants analyse situations related to online behaviour, consent, visibility, inclusion and responsibility. Rather than focusing on rules, the activity encourages reasoning, empathy and collective reflection. Participants explore how different perspectives influence ethical decisions and how digital actions may affect individuals, communities and public dialogue. By discussing real-life dilemmas, young people learn that digital citizenship is not a checklist of rules but an ongoing process of reflection and responsible decision-making. By the end of the activity, participants will be able to: 1.identify ethical challenges in everyday digital situations 2.reflect on the consequences of online actions on individuals and groups 3.practise respectful dialogue and collective decision-making 4.strengthen awareness of inclusion, consent and responsibility in digital participation. Warm-up: Ethics is everywhere (~10 min) Facilitator starts with short questions: Have you ever hesitated before posting something? Have you ever seen content that felt “wrong” but wasn’t illegal? Who decides what is acceptable online? Participants are reminded that ethical issues often appear in ordinary digital moments, not extreme cases. Digital dilemma groups (~15 min) Participants are divided into small groups (4–5 people). Each group receives one digital dilemma scenario, such as: Example dilemmas: A photo from a youth activity is shared online without asking everyone in the picture. A group chat starts mocking someone who is not present. An influencer spreads misleading information but claims it’s “just an opinion”. A youth platform allows anonymous comments that sometimes become harmful. Ethical Analysis (~25 min) Groups discuss their dilemma using guiding questions: Who is involved or affected? What rights are at stake? Who has power in this situation? What could be the short- and long-term consequences? What would be an ethical response? There is no pressure to agree immediately. Decision & Justification (~20 min) Each group prepares: One proposed response or decision A short explanation of why they chose it Groups present their dilemma and solution to others. Whole group reflection (~20 min) Facilitated discussion focusing on: Differences between group responses Tensions between freedom and responsibility How inclusion or exclusion appears in digital spaces How digital participation can be both empowering and harmful Facilitator highlights that ethical citizenship is a process, not a checklist. Methodology Steps: Digital dilemmas lab 131 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 90 min. 132 ANNEX Digital dilemmas lab Annex: Digital dilemma cards . 5 Card 1 A student uses AI to write almost an entire essay and submits it as their own work. The final text is excellent and the teacher never notices. Is this cheating? Card 2 A friend shares screenshots from a private conversation in a group chat because they think the messages are funny. The person in the screenshots never gave permission. Is it acceptable? Card 3 An influencer shares false information online but insists that it is \"just their opinion\" and everyone has the right to express themselves. Should they be held responsible? Card 4 A video of a participant making a mistake during a youth activity is posted online and quickly gains attention. Many people find it funny, but the participant feels embarrassed. Should the video stay online? The following cards may be used during the Digital Dilemmas Lab activity. Facilitators are encouraged to adapt the scenarios according to the age, background and interests of participants. Card 5 A content creator heavily edits their photos and promotes an unrealistic lifestyle. They never claim the images are real. Is this misleading or simply creative expression? Card 6 A youth platform allows anonymous comments. While anonymity helps some users express themselves freely, it also leads to insults and harassment. Should anonymous commenting be removed? Card 7 A meme about a particular nationality or cultural group becomes popular online. Many people see it as harmless humour, while others consider it offensive. Where should the line be drawn? Card 8 Important information about a community event is shared only through social media. Several young people who do not use these platforms miss the opportunity to participate. Is this fair? + | - 1 - - Y O U - "},{"u":"chapter-5-activity-4.html","t":"Activity 4 · Inclusive digital participation lab","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":" Material list 1.Concrete inclusion strategies for digital participation 2.Increased awareness of power and visibility in digital spaces 3.Strengthened sense of collective responsibility 4.Practical ideas transferable to youth work practice Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Reference Inclusive youth work methodologies, participatory digital citizenship practices, and non-formal education approaches to civic engagement. Flipchart papers Markers Sticky notes (2–3 colours) Printed participation role cards (optional) Overview Recommended: 80 minutes Extended: 1 h 40 min Which barriers surprised you the most? How can small design choices increase inclusion? What responsibilities do we have when creating digital spaces? How does inclusion strengthen digital citizenship? Learning objectives This activity helps young people explore how digital spaces can be designed for inclusive participation. Rather than focusing on individual behaviour, it examines who is visible, heard or excluded online. Participants identify barriers to participation and develop strategies to create safer, more accessible environments. The focus is on how digital spaces are structured and moderated, highlighting the shared responsibility to build inclusive online communities. By the end of the activity, participants will be able to: 1.recognise barriers that limit inclusion in digital participation 2.reflect on whose voices are amplified, ignored or marginalised online 3.design practical strategies that encourage safer and more inclusive participation in digital spaces 4.understand digital citizenship not only as individual behaviour but also as the collective design of fair and inclusive digital environments. Mapping digital participation (~15 min) The facilitator invites participants to reflect individually: Where do young people usually participate digitally? Who tends to create content or speak online? Who tends to remain silent or invisible? Participants write their answers on sticky notes and place them on a shared board. The facilitator clusters responses to visualise patterns of visibility and participation. Who is missing? (~20 min) In small groups, participants discuss: Which groups are often excluded or underrepresented online? Why might they not participate? Possible barriers may include: confidence and fear of judgement, language barriers, limited digital access or skills, online harassment, lack of representation Groups categorise these barriers into: structural barriers, social barriers, psychological barriers Designing inclusive participation (~30 min) Each group selects one digital context, for example: an online youth platform, a social media campaign, a digital community space, an online discussion forum Groups design 3–5 strategies that would make participation more inclusive, answering: How can participation feel safer?, How can different voices be encouraged?, How can power and visibility be shared?, How can feedback and respectful dialogue be supported? Gallery walk & peer exchange (~15–20 min) Groups display their strategies on flipcharts. Participants walk around the room, read other groups’ ideas and add comments or suggestions using sticky notes. The facilitator encourages participants to notice: similar ideas, creative solutions, new perspectives on participation Reflection & Transfer (~15–20 min) A whole-group reflection explores: What makes digital participation meaningful? What role do facilitators and peers play in inclusion? How can digital spaces unintentionally exclude people? How can these strategies be applied in youth work settings? The facilitator highlights that inclusive participation does not happen automatically-it requires intentional design and shared responsibility. Methodology Steps: Inclusive digital participation lab 133 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - 80 min. "},{"u":"chapter-5-activity-5.html","t":"Activity 5 · Community opportunity lab","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":" Material list 1.Opportunity maps identifying local challenges or cultural opportunities. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Reference EntreComp framework Large paper sheets or whiteboard Sticky notes Markers Community maps (optional) Project idea templates Overview Recommended: 4 hours Extended: 5 hours Can be split into two sessions. Intro – 35 min Mapping – 55 min Idea Sprint – 70 min Pitching – 55 min Reflection – 25 min What opportunities in your community are currently overlooked? How can youth workers help young people identify such opportunities? Which ideas have potential to grow into real projects? Learning objectives Participants explore their local community to identify opportunities where culture, heritage, or digital innovation could create value. Instead of starting from business ideas, the workshop begins with observing real needs, gaps, or underused cultural resources. Youth workers learn how to guide young participants through opportunity discovery and idea generation. By the end of the activity, participants will be able to: 1.identify community needs and opportunities related to culture or youth participation 2.practise opportunity recognition, a key entrepreneurial competence 3.develop initial ideas for youth-led projects or initiatives 4.encourage observation, curiosity, and creative thinking. Introduction & Inspiration (~35 min) The facilitator introduces entrepreneurial thinking and opportunity recognition. Participants discuss examples of youth-led initiatives or community projects that addressed a local need. Community mapping exercise (~55 min) Participants work in small teams and create a map of opportunities in their community, identifying: cultural places or heritage sites unused community spaces underrepresented stories or traditions youth needs related to culture or employment Idea generation sprint (~70 min) Teams transform one opportunity into a potential project idea. They define the problem or need, who benefits, what solution they propose and how digital tools might help Pitch & Discussion (~55 min) Teams present their ideas in 3-minute pitches. The group discusses feasibility, impact, and potential next steps. Reflection (~25 min) Participants reflect on the opportunity recognition process and how it could be applied in youth work. Methodology Steps: Community opportunity lab 134 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - hrs "},{"u":"chapter-5-activity-6.html","t":"Activity 6 · Entrepreneurial pitch for cultural projects","c":"Chapter 5 · Digital Citizenship","x":" Material list A short entrepreneurial pitch introducing a cultural or youth- focused project idea. This output helps participants practise communicating ideas clearly while linking creativity, cultural initiatives and entrepreneurial thinking. Estimated timing Expected outputs Assessment & reflection Reference Inclusive youth work methodologies, participatory digital citizenship practices, and non-formal education approaches to civic engagement. Pitch templates Notebook and pens Timer Overview Recommended: 4 hours Can be split into two sessions. Intro – 30 min Story structure – 60 min Pitch practice – 65 min Pitch session – 60 min Reflection – 25 min What makes a project story engaging and convincing? How can storytelling help young people promote cultural initiatives? What communication skills are most important for youth entrepreneurs? Learning objectives Participants learn how to communicate entrepreneurial ideas through storytelling and pitching, an essential skill when promoting projects, seeking partners, or applying for funding. The activity highlights that communicating a project is not only about persuasion, but also about responsibility, transparency and ethical representation of ideas in digital and public spaces. By the end of the activity, participants will be able to: 1.understand how storytelling supports entrepreneurship 2.develop persuasive communication skills 3.practise presenting project ideas clearly 4.learn how to communicate cultural initiatives to wider audiences 5.communicate ideas responsibly and transparently 6.consider the social and ethical impact of their projects. Introduction to storytelling in entrepreneurship (~30 min) Facilitator explains why storytelling is important for entrepreneurs and project leaders. Key elements: Problem, vision, solution, impact Story structure exercise (~60 min) Participants choose a project idea and build a story structure explaining it. They outline: The problem, the idea, who benefits and its expected impact Pitch Practice (~65 min) Participants prepare a 2-minute storytelling pitch. They practise in pairs and provide feedback. Public pitch session (~60 min) Volunteers present their stories to the group. Reflection & Discussion (~25 min) Participants reflect on communication challenges and effective storytelling strategies. Methodology Steps: Entrepreneurial pitch for cultural projects 135 . + | - 1 - - Y O U - From reflection to action Choose one digital action you would like to adopt, change or develop after this workshop. 4 hrs "},{"u":"facilitator-tips.html#content","t":"Facilitator tips","c":"Facilitator tips","x":"Download the facilitator tips (PDF) These tips come from the experience of facilitators who tested the workshops with young people across the partner countries. They are not strict rules. Think of them as practical advice to help you create a safe, open and engaging space , adapt the activities to your group, and get the most out of each session. Read them before you facilitate, and come back to them whenever you need inspiration."},{"u":"facilitator-tips.html#facilitator-s-role-sintro","t":"Facilitator's role","c":"Facilitator tips","x":"Focus on guiding rather than teaching . Use questions to help participants explore ideas, experiences and perspectives."},{"u":"facilitator-tips.html#lead-by-example-sintro","t":"Lead by example","c":"Facilitator tips","x":"Participants often understand the task much faster after hearing a personal example . Sharing your own experience can reduce uncertainty and encourage more open participation."},{"u":"facilitator-tips.html#culture-iceberg-sintro","t":"Culture iceberg","c":"Facilitator tips","x":"Before starting the activity, invite participants to introduce themselves through three simple questions : Who are you? What connects you most to your culture? What do you hope to gain from this workshop?"},{"u":"facilitator-tips.html#cultural-diversity-exists-within-countries-sintro","t":"Cultural diversity exists within countries","c":"Facilitator tips","x":"If participants come from the same country, explore differences in regions, cities, languages, traditions and local identities. Cultural diversity can often be found within communities , not only between countries."},{"u":"facilitator-tips.html#digital-dilemmas-lab-sintro","t":"Digital dilemmas lab","c":"Facilitator tips","x":"Use dilemma cards that are clear, diverse and relevant to participants' everyday digital experiences. Realistic and occasionally controversial scenarios often generate richer discussions."},{"u":"facilitator-tips.html#read-the-energy-of-the-group-sintro","t":"Read the energy of the group","c":"Facilitator tips","x":"Be ready to adapt the pace and depth of activities to the group's energy and engagement levels. Some groups need more discussion and reflection, while others respond better to shorter explanations and more interaction."},{"u":"facilitator-tips.html#silence-is-okay-sintro","t":"Silence is okay","c":"Facilitator tips","x":"Do not rush to fill moments of silence. Giving participants time to think often leads to deeper and more thoughtful responses."},{"u":"facilitator-tips.html#meaningful-reflection-sintro","t":"Meaningful reflection","c":"Facilitator tips","x":"At the end of the workshop, revisit the expectations participants shared at the beginning. Ask what they gained, what surprised them, what they found most meaningful and what they will take with them after the workshop."},{"u":"conclusion.html#content","t":"Conclusion of the guide","c":"Conclusion of the guide","x":"Throughout this guide, we have explored a learning journey that ranges from understanding digital environments to actively engaging with them through culture, storytelling and participation. Young people are not merely users of digital tools . They are also observers, creators, storytellers and citizens. They become cultural ambassadors , representing their communities and promoting their cultures to a wider audience. By helping them explore their cultural environment, gather testimonies, develop narratives and share them responsibly , youth workers can play a vital role in promoting inclusive and meaningful digital practices. This guide also highlights a key point: digital skills are not merely technical ; they are also critical, cultural and ethical ."},{"u":"conclusion.html#an-ongoing-process-of-learning-and-adaptation-sintro","t":"An ongoing process of learning and adaptation","c":"Conclusion of the guide","x":"Digital and media literacy education cannot be fixed or static. It requires an ongoing process of: questioning and critical thinking adapting to new tools and practices reflection on the impact of digital practices openness to new forms of expression and participation Youth workers and educators are therefore not merely facilitators of knowledge, but learners in their own right , evolving alongside the young people they support."},{"u":"conclusion.html#promoting-responsible-and-meaningful-cultural-participation-sintro","t":"Promoting responsible and meaningful cultural participation","c":"Conclusion of the guide","x":"In a digital world where content is produced and shared on an unprecedented scale, the question is no longer just how to create, but also why and how to share . Promoting responsible digital practices means encouraging young people to: create intentionally rather than react impulsively prioritise quality over quantity respect the views and cultural diversity of others consider the impact of their digital actions on communities and the environment. Approached in this way, cultural storytelling becomes a powerful tool for connecting individuals, communities and stories , and for fostering dialogue and mutual understanding."},{"u":"conclusion.html#outlook-sintro","t":"Outlook","c":"Conclusion of the guide","x":"The practices outlined in this guide are not an end in themselves, but a starting point . Now it’s your turn to embark on the OurCulture adventure: experiment adapt the methods to different contexts co-create with young people keep developing new ways of engaging with digital culture through cultural exploration and responsible participation visit the OurCulture platform and check out the creations by young people from France, Hungary and Turkey; like and comment invite young people to contribute and share the content they’ve created. Youth work and engagement can help shape a digital space that is not only more informed, but also more inclusive, creative and respectful . From this perspective, every story shared responsibly becomes a means of preserving cultural heritage , expressing one’s identity and forging links between communities."},{"u":"conclusion.html#authors","t":"Authors","c":"Conclusion of the guide","x":"FRANCE Eutopique Laurence Bertea-Granet Elodie Perreau Alienor Turpin Emmanuelle Ibara Enora Roy TURKEY Akdeniz Panorama Derneği Emin Peker Aysar Güven Songül Gören Kiraz Yildiz Peker Handegül Yildiz Mehmet Esat Özdemir Fidan Yildiz Özdemir Safiye Ebrar Etiz HUNGARY Fiatalok YouthTogether Laura Casaiz Trinchete Aykhan Samadov Graphic design: Limbus Studio Cover and back cover: original graphic design of the OurCulture project. Photographs and illustrations: © Adobe Stock, used under licence. Photos © Elodie Perreau, P.50 and 111, with the consent of the people photographed. The photographs are not covered by the Creative Commons licence of the guide and cannot be reused separately."},{"u":"index.html#resources","t":"Resources to download","c":"Home","x":"The complete guide. Digital Transversal Skills Action Guide, all chapters, activities and case studies. All activity sheets. The 23 ready-to-use activity sheets of the five chapters, in one PDF or as separate files. The five infographics. The visual summary of each chapter, in one PDF. Facilitator tips. Practical advice from the facilitators who tested the workshops. Photo, video & sound consent form. To print and have signed before recording participants. Practical digital safety checklist. The full extended checklist for youth, with guidance and exercises. European needs analysis. The project’s global research report (survey and focus groups). Itinerary interview cards. Three sets of interview cards (an object, a tradition, a monument), inspired by the itinerary method of anthropologist Dominique Desjeux."}];